window.harnessChunks["rows-992a8c51f446a1ee.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T20:55:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rohaquinlop/michin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MichiN</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rohaquinlop/michin/tree/ff001031aad8486583055455989e02d2a88c8518\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ff001031aad8486583055455989e02d2a88c8518\">ff00103 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute first-party model-selected source and shell tools for project coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user requirements, source state, compile errors, returned tool failures and environmental constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select contingent source-edit/read/search/bash actions and decide subsequent work after observing outcomes. - Decision owner: The coding LLM operating through MichiN&#39;s own Agent/loop, with action rights bounded by native tool/policy enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider streaming, native tool dispatcher, guardrails, context history, session state and user approval/mode choices. - Closure path: User coding request → inference chooses tool action → first-party native tool executes on workspace → returned result enters core state → model chooses next corrective action or finishes. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI, TUI, prompt and RPC operation call the first-party Rust agent core and first-party concrete tools. External providers supply inference only, not an external coding harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides how to absorb local coding variety; native tools and policies enforce its chosen bounded work. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs; crates/michin/src/tools/write.rs; crates/michin/src/tools/bash.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is evidence of operative autonomy, not a measured benchmark-quality claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party organizational coordination loop across two independently functioning operational S1 cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent tool execution may affect one active coding session, but does not evidence competing autonomous work units whose variety must be mutually stabilized. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None across distinct S1 units; generic tool execution mode is selected/obeyed inside the single coder. - Decision owner: No separate S2 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Parallel or sequential native tool execution, queued user messages, session-level cancellation. - Closure path: Tool output returns to the one model/agent; no inter-S1 interference detection and feedback to another S1 is seen. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parallel file reads, serialized edit calls, model switching and queueing do not constitute an inter-agent coordination relationship. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Separately running MichiN processes would need a separately established first-party organizational connection.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned whole-current regulator that supervises and reallocates work across multiple operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Run interruption, latency, current budget, repeated model/tool requests and execution errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User steers or cancels; native runtime enforces timeout/repetition bounds and local safety policy. - Decision owner: Deterministic single-session runtime and human task operator, not an S3 actor with discretionary whole-current view. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Active-run mutex, cancellation tokens, run reports, tool watchdog, turn termination statuses and model escalation. - Closure path: Stop/steer/escalate changes this agent&#39;s present tool turn, without manager-mediated system-wide current allocation across multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Status reports, execution ceilings and individual agent control do not transfer broader managerial judgment to the model. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/events.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External human management of a coding team lies outside this packaged local session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material independent operational-audit owner with corrective return to coding S1. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe commands and possibly defective code require checks, but safety enforcement does not itself establish complementary audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic command safety rejects destructive actions; optional Rhai scripts can pre-block or post-observe tools, not act as a separately autonomous supplementary auditor by default. - Decision owner: Static runtime policy and user-configured scripts; original coder retains responsibility for correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Before/after tool hooks, Rhai executor, command policy engine and tool result events. - Closure path: Hook/policy may block one tool, or script observes a result; errors return to original coding context. No separate source-reading auditor returns independent defect judgment as binding corrective work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Custom hooks, a second plan model and tool guards are not automatically complementary audit actors. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin-script/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A separately supplied Rhai extension might implement richer audit logic, but its hypothetical behavior cannot be borrowed as default first-party capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment analysis and strategic organizational capability renewal authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-horizon external requirements and future tool/provider evolution are not addressed by one-session task planning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User selects model/plan track, preserves skills and restores sessions; the single coding agent performs local plans. - Decision owner: No first-party prospective S4 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan and build model tracks, context compaction, persistent session forks, resources and skills. - Closure path: Settings and notes guide later coding turns, but no future-environment options are examined and accepted into organizational capabilities by a strategic owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory, switching to a better model and proposing a coding plan are not standalone future-environment intelligence. - Evidence: crates/michin/src/settings.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/compact.rs; crates/michin/src/skills.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer research/roadmap decisions are outside installed runtime ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No constitutive identity/ultimate-purpose policy authority with organizational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permission risk and destructive commands are local execution safety matters, not highest-order identity/policy conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User configures policies and model mode; runtime follows explicit forbidden-command constraints. - Decision owner: Human operator for task settings and deterministic guard for enforcement; no legitimate highest-level first-party actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Command policy, strict safety flags, Rhai allow/block callbacks and plan-mode mutation gate. - Closure path: Tool permitted/refused or model track changed; no ultimate organizational identity-policy decision returns to regulate the whole operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Constraints and human approvals do not establish an autonomous S5 decision right. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin/src/config.rs; crates/michin/src/settings.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: OSS repository governance, licensing and developer policy are adjacent to user coding session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5e6d9710b191304c3ba49f7a9abe9f75447e3f50/assessments/michin.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5e6d9710b191304c3ba49f7a9abe9f75447e3f50\">5e6d971 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T21:40:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fennec-AI/agent-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fennec Agent Runtime</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fennec-AI/agent-runtime/tree/3441fbde350df50cc070851856c343680babefb4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3441fbde350df50cc070851856c343680babefb4\">3441fbd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select model-guided actions and tool arguments for an assigned user task, observe their effects or errors and iteratively choose the next action or terminal response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novel user instructions, file/tool observations, tool execution errors, rejected permissions, child task results and context-length growth. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ctx.llm model in the shipped query loop emits toolcalls or final text and is fed the actual ToolMessage and child-inbox observations on the next iteration. - Decision owner: Configured model acting through first-party Session/ query and runtools boundary; LangChain/provider services transport inference and host sets tool grants but do not displace model selection of the next substantive task action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party registry and RunContext, safety-filtered tool adapter, BaseChatModel.bindtools, tool executor, child notifications, compaction, cancellation, events, user approval callback and typed terminal result facility. - Closure path: Task → Session.submitmessage / query → llm.bindtools(...).astream → model-selected tool → runtools permission check and tool.ainvoke → ToolMessage in history or child completion notification → same model re-evaluates → terminal result. - Boundary reachability: The public Session facade invokes the shipped query and its tool-dispatch branch; query feeds concrete returned tool results into the next model call and SpawnAgentTool reuses it for children. The README quickstart connects the actual packaged Session, bootstrap, defaulttools() and provider model, not an externally invented loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model, the first-party tool dispatcher and safety infrastructure can execute preselected actions but cannot choose task-sensitive tool calls from new observations. The model owns the material S1 decision, bounded by host-selected tool grants. - Evidence: README.md#L72, agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/session.py#L89, agentruntime/query.py#L110, agentruntime/query.py#L181, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L102, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/tools/spawnagent.py#L83. - Basis: Concrete shipped model-call/tool-output return wiring, not extrapolation from README feature labels alone. - Confidence: High for source-connected operating capability; no local provider inference validation. - Caveats: External LangChain/provider and valid API key/runtime dependencies are needed to execute real tasks; a source-based A does not imply measured reliability or autonomy beyond the configured operating cell.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate source-backed regulator of specific interference/oscillation between independently viable S1 operating divisions with altered reciprocal operating commitments is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent child tasks may compete for a shared worktree or return conflicting content; async notifications may arrive during parent streaming, but those issues are not the same as interference between independent business S1 systems. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Parent operating model selects subtask calls and may send messages, poll or cancel; runtime has deterministic safe-tool batching and inbox delivery. Neither path senses concrete inter-S1 department conflict, decides dampening, and returns binding mutual adjustment to two viable operating units. - Decision owner: Same parent operating model / host task instructions for delegation, process-local routing and task cancellation code for transport/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SpawnAgentTool, TaskOutput, TaskStop, SendMessage, AGENTREGISTRY, inbox drain and concurrency semaphore, cooperative cancellation. - Closure path: Parent delegates → child returns or notifies → parent continues current user task; no multi-S1 shared operational interference → distinct coordinating judgment → changed independent operating commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model mediation of child tasks is still the parent S1 operating decision. A messenger, task graph or transport does not automatically confer the S2 function. - Evidence: agentruntime/tools/spawnagent.py#L1, agentruntime/query.py#L143, agentruntime/query.py#L210, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L102, agentruntime/registry.py#L1. - Basis: Source-bounded negative after inspecting real child task, process-inbox and safe-batch modes. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared one-goal operating recursion. - Caveats: A downstream application could install multiple independently viable S1 agents and new conflict-regulating logic; it would require its own evidence and recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current management owner of a portfolio of independently viable S1 operations that reviews present obligations/resources and sends binding managerial adjustments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Depth/max-turn constraints, cancellation, shell timeout, active child tasks, approval and context budget threaten single-task execution, but not whole-organizational current performance accountability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/host configures permission mode, tools, model and maxturns; parent model selects child task instructions, and runtime applies fixed deterministic run limits. No source-backed separate managerial owner negotiates resource allocations or current organizational commitments. - Decision owner: Application operator for delegated bounds and parent operating model for subtask choices; deterministic runtime for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session, RunContext, CONFIG, TaskStop, abort tree, registry, permission callback and structured output checking. - Closure path: Task or tool requests action → configured permission/depth/abort gate applies → same task continues or ends. No whole-current manager&#39;s operating overview → change in S1 portfolio priorities/resources → returned operational adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A class called Session or parent worker does not by itself own discretionary current management of a viable multi-unit organization. - Evidence: agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/query.py#L102, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/cancellation.py#L1, agentruntime/config.py#L1. - Basis: Source-bounded negative for registered source modes, including child and approval routes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Human S3 or autonomous management could exist externally at a higher application boundary; not imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary operational-reality inspection of S1/S3 claims with a corrective feedback path to a current S3 executive is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool errors, unsafe actions and malformed typed answers are observable and may be denied or retried. Those observations do not constitute independent challenge of an S3 operating picture. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluatepermission may call an external approval callback; hooks log/observe tool use; structured output Pydantic validation checks schema during the same task. No separate first-party S3 correction mandate is wired. - Decision owner: Host approval callback and deterministic tool/typed response checks; not a complementary independent auditor with rights over a current S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool-level pretool/ posttool/ onerror hooks, permissiondenied events, runstructured, transcript history, registry/cancellation. - Closure path: Tool result or approval decision → returned ToolMessage / validation action / log → current S1 loop; no independent reality sample → corrective S3 audit finding → revised S3 current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission review and output validation are local action controls, not S3 regardless of their security value. - Evidence: agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/permissions.py#L1, agentruntime/hooks.py#L1, agentruntime/session.py#L107. - Basis: Function-first negative: checked concrete validation and hook/approval closure. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A downstream independent evidence checker could leverage the logs, but no such authority is credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No model-owned proactive future environment distinction, prospective organizational adaptation option and adoption back to changed durable future operating capability is evidenced in the shipped default or alternate documented modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long conversations, differing tool results and future continuation after a child completes can require local context adjustment, but not a durable new strategy for a later independent operational cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autocompactifneeded may ask the configured model to summarize the current history and replace messages at a context threshold; Session persists current messages; TodoWrite updates current task checklist. None owns prospective changes to installed tools, goals, model policies or future agent skill capabilities. - Decision owner: Same operating loop&#39;s compaction / task model and application for any later configuration changes; no distinct S4 adaptation right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compaction/autocompact.py, microcompact.py, token budget, messages and action history, child completion notifications, TODO list tool. - Closure path: Growing current transcript → compact summary in same messages list → same model continues current task; missing prospective future environmental distinction → approved strategy change → reused operational capability loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-written conversation summaries are still local operating-context maintenance, not strategy ownership. No first-party future-option development and adoption is returned into later independent runs. - Evidence: agentruntime/query.py#L74, agentruntime/compaction/autocompact.py#L47, agentruntime/session.py#L89, agentruntime/tools/todowrite.py#L1, README.md#L24. - Basis: Bounded negative after reviewing persistent session/child and compaction modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Host developers could expose a model-written durable skill/update tool, but that would be a new application-owned construction and is not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate first-party ultimate-purpose/identity decision authority with returned enduring binding organizational policy is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potentially destructive shell/file tools, elevated permission modes and background subagent lifecycle require user approval/limits, but no constitutional identity-level dispute is admitted or decided. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host user supplies permission mode and optional askcallback; runtime checks allow/deny/ask for tool actions. bypassPermissions is a caller-selected setting, not first-party S5 policy-making authority. - Decision owner: External user/application actor for authorization; deterministic runtime evaluates delegated boundaries and operating model chooses permitted task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionMode, AskCallback, per-tool allow/deny, tool/child cancellation, logs and optional hooks. - Closure path: Tool requests action → policy gate/host callback → tool executes or denial returned to current agent; no ultimate identity question reaches a legitimate authority and returns as binding constitutional policy governing future task contexts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even a high-friction human gate is not itself a functioning organizational S5 policy/identity constitution. - Evidence: agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/permissions.py#L1, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, README.md#L31. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking all named permission modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External human owners retain real final purpose/policy rights; no internally self-deciding S5 organization is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/13dab0d4f2954614f7f99a9fa044ad5b9a154490/assessments/fe/fennec-agent-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 13dab0d4f2954614f7f99a9fa044ad5b9a154490\">13dab0d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T19:10:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bottrall/riffer-rig\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">riffer-rig</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bottrall/riffer-rig/tree/e2e914195f7c15c75ee2ae6bb55d772d754a2f37\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e2e914195f7c15c75ee2ae6bb55d772d754a2f37\">e2e9141 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding work through model-selected repository inspection, edits, writes and shell execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, incomplete task information, file state, failing commands/tests, ambiguous edit targets, model/provider outputs and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next coding/tool action, interpret tool errors/results and revise subsequent actions until the requested coding outcome is reached. - Decision owner: the active model-backed riffer-rig CodingAgent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CodingAgent, generic-agent execution instantiated by the rig, read/write/edit/bash tools, REPL streaming, runtime cancel flag, hooks/guardrails, AGENTS.md prompt section and provider/model configuration. - Closure path: user/project context → model selects action/tool → tool executes against the working directory → result/error returns to the agent context → model changes subsequent action until completion or cancellation. - Boundary reachability: the installed riffer executable directly instantiates the repository-owned CodingAgent and bundled coding tools; no adjacent development workflow or unconfigured generic runtime feature is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves tools, UI and runtime wrappers but removes the open-ended decisions that turn repository evidence into coding actions. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/codingagent.rb; lib/riffer/rig/repl.rb; lib/riffer/rig/tools/read.rb; write.rb; edit.rb; bash.rb. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the generic riffer execution kernel is a dependency; only behavior concretely instantiated by the frozen riffer-rig composition is credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct coordination function among multiple S1 operational units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the focal runtime may execute tools and load extensions/skills, but the standard coding application exposes one model-backed coding unit rather than peer S1 units with an interference relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific choice over inter-S1 conflict, oscillation or mutual adjustment was found. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime busy state, cancellation, extension ordering and per-runtime working directory. - Closure path: these mechanisms constrain one operational session and do not feed a coordination result back into distinct peer S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: serialization, extension ordering and tool composition do not establish S2 without a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/runtime.rb; docs/TOOLS.md; docs/SESSIONS.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: embedders can construct multiple runtimes externally, but the frozen first-party rig does not supply a peer-coordination relation among them.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cancellation, runtime busy/closed state, tool allowlists and request/tool guardrails constrain current execution within one session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate owner has a whole-system current view and substantive authority over shared resources, commitments, priorities or interventions across multiple operations. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime busy/closed state, cancel flag, request/tool guardrails, hooks, model switching and host notifications. - Closure path: these controls alter or stop the focal session&#39;s current action path but do not form a whole-current metasystemic loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: local execution controls and guardrails are enforcement around S1, not whole-system management. - Evidence: lib/riffer/rig/runtime.rb; runtime/requestguardrail.rb; runtime hooks; REPL model switching. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an embedding application could add fleet management, but such an adjacent composition is outside this concrete coding rig.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool results, command/test failures, hooks and repository tests can reveal mistakes, but they remain ordinary production feedback or adjacent development checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate reviewer/evaluator owns an independent claim-checking judgment that returns findings into a current-control path. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell/test output, after-tool hooks, notifications and repository test suite. - Closure path: runtime evidence returns to the same coding agent or host; no distinct complementary-access audit loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-checking via bash/tests and hook observation are ordinary operational evidence, not S3. - Evidence: lib/riffer/rig/tools/bash.rb; runtime hooks/events; repository tests. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an extension could implement review behavior, but no such first-party review path is instantiated in the frozen standard rig.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AGENTS.md, user/project skills, provider/model settings and snapshots can change the context used by later turns or restored sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party process senses environmental/future change, generates adaptation options for the rig and returns a selected adaptation into current capability. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live AGENTS.md loading, filesystem skill backend, model/provider configuration and runtime snapshot/restore. - Closure path: operator-authored instructions/configuration or saved session state are loaded into later operation; no autonomous external-and-prospective adaptation conversation exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context loading and extensibility reuse externally supplied material but do not autonomously develop future-oriented adaptation options. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/prompts/agentsmd.rb; lib/riffer/rig/codingagent.rb; docs/SESSIONS.md; settings.rb. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: skills may encode reusable procedures, but this repository does not supply a learning/evolution loop that creates or revises them from environmental evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AGENTS.md instructions, tool allowlists, hooks/guardrails, settings and provider credentials constrain ordinary execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a durable governing decision for the harness. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: instruction hierarchy, request/tool guardrails, tool allowlists, settings and credentials. - Closure path: constraints affect current prompts/tool calls/configuration but do not close identity-level policy questions for subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static/configurable prompts and guardrails are execution policy mechanisms, not S5. - Evidence: prompts/agentsmd.rb; runtime/requestguardrail.rb; runtime.rb; settings.rb; README. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project maintainers and users can change instructions/configuration outside the run, but that adjacent authority is not a first-party S5 runtime loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/03ba69b67f5d1b4947c68a70e1515f45faaa64f1/assessments/riffer-rig.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 03ba69b67f5d1b4947c68a70e1515f45faaa64f1\">03ba69b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-21T16:49:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/markmhendrickson/ateles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ateles</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-21\">2026-10-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/markmhendrickson/ateles/tree/516edbf0bf6a96654f69b8a797fd2998aa4983cb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 516edbf0bf6a96654f69b8a797fd2998aa4983cb\">516edbf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Agent work cells perform model-guided coding, reviews, release handling, mail/calendar/content tasks in the configured personal swarm. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended task requirements and changing tool/work results, including code defects, review findings and external requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The loaded agent/skill chooses task-semantic tool actions and adapts during a headless Codex/Claude/Cursor execution; specific risky outward effects are separately gated. - Decision owner: Each dispatched, role-defined model agent within an Ateles-controlled T4 invocation; external model inference alone is not assigned supervisory authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Neotoma agentdefinition mirror/loader, signed grants, provider selector, context/system prompt, controlled subprocess, task/session records, event dispatch. - Closure path: Event/task arrives → Apis/Anthus selects role and builds scoped prompt → first-party runner launches model tool loop → result/agent-produced outputs are recorded to Neotoma, can enter follow-up workflow and approved external action. - Boundary reachability: execution/daemons/apis/apis.py::spawnharnessskill() calls shipped skillrunner.runskill(); runner constructs actual CLI commands and subprocesses for named agents, and anthus.py::spawnagent() provides another wired route. External inference is a configured dependency for shipped first-party autonomous work cell, not a separately administered employee. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The roles&#39; model-driven semantic choices and tool paths are bounded by first-party orchestration, identity and task/result recording. Removing the model leaves deterministic dispatch but no S1 problem-solving; keeping it in this shipped path realizes S1. - Evidence: api; runner; anthus; prereq; readme - Basis: structural and explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent damaging overlap between distinct S1 agent builds targeting the same repository issue and return deferred work into task execution when the collision ceases. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independent dispatched work cells can act on the same repository#issue before the first completes; uncoordinated duplicate editing/PR delivery would cause interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which in-flight build retains the issue key, hold the overlapping task with an exact deferral record, and release/re-dispatch eligible deferred work when the claim ends. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party Apis/builddelivery machinery owns atomic in-process claim and recovery; task agents execute after the coordination decision, but there is no demonstrated agent discretion in choosing the coordination policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activebuild, claimbuild, deferralcontext, guarded Neotoma task writes, release hook, startup sweep, deferred task recovery and scoped worktree. - Closure path: A second task finds an active issue claim and becomes a durable deferredbehindbuild task; the original holder&#39;s release signals a recovery sweep, which checks fresh state and re-dispatches only qualified work. Different task agents then resume in the permitted order. - Boundary reachability: apis.py&#39;s task dispatcher and recovery paths use builddelivery and skillrunner.runskill; these are shipped Python modules, not foundation-only future requirements. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The code establishes a concrete inter-S1 collision and specific attenuation with feedback; C recognizes this first-party function-specific coordination construction without pretending the model agent chooses the rules. - Evidence: delivery; api; runner - Basis: structural - Confidence: moderate-high - Caveats: The in-flight claim is explicitly process-local, not a distributed lease among independently deployed operator instances. Broader design claims are not inherited.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: ? - Function: Whole-current swarm regulation over task priorities, resource limits, external-effect commitments and corrective intervention is plausible but not fully evidenced as one organization-level S3 decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cross-domain execution costs, asynchronous task backlog, delivery collisions and approval risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A whole-system authority should choose/revise operational commitments and priorities using a global view and return changes to S1 agent work. - Decision owner: The operator controls some execution policy, while deterministic dispatch gates constrain tasks; the evidence does not yet establish a single decisive current-control owner and closed whole-system feedback path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Blast/confidence gate, role grants, configurable model tier, task queue, checkpoints, provider capacity windows and reports. - Closure path: Per-action approval/checkpoint and per-task retry are implemented, but a sourced whole-swarm performance/resource bargaining and accountable reallocation loop was not reconstructed. - Boundary reachability: No positive S3 published; the first-party task gate and current-task control paths remain plausible and prevent a bounded —. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A threshold gate and routing/scheduling are not by themselves global resource regulation; the need to inspect live, accepted priorities and operator override return paths is material. - Evidence: policy; api; arch; status - Basis: structural with unresolved semantics -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate complementary, sufficiently independent audit judgment with findings returned into a current S3 management decision is established for the shipped swarm. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Untruthful task completion, inaccurate recorded evidence or mistaken routine checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct, independent access path must challenge normal S1/S3 reporting and feed corrective control. - Decision owner: Routine reviewers, workflow sign-offs, CI checks and Neotoma audit records exist; no function-specific complementary auditor/decisive feedback owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Signed identities, observation provenance, developer/tests, review gates, operator checkpoints, archive and drift records. - Closure path: Ordinary review/gate findings can block one task, but a distinct sporadic audit channel + corrective whole-system management return is not shown. - Boundary reachability: No positive state claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate reviewer role, signature or audit trail alone cannot establish S3 at the assessed agent-organization recursion. - Evidence: status; gates; arch; general - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Convert externally relevant runtime/organizational drift and contradictions into prospective policy-adjustment options for later authorization and operational adoption. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New conditions, recurring failures, tool/provider behaviours, misfit of agent operating rules against task environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Distinguish repeated environmental drift, propose changed agent policy, and present an adaptation option to governing authority for incorporation into future operating rules. - Decision owner: The first-party generalizer clusters drift and creates signed agentpolicy/revision proposals; ultimate active-policy authorization is left to the operator/approval path, with no self-promotion of live rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Anthus drift harvesting, threshold/risk classification, operator-conflict detection, signed proposal write, Neotoma-backed policy reader. - Closure path: anthus harvests drift → generalizer.processsignals classifies clusters and persists policy/revision proposals → separately authorized operator must approve/adopt → live agentpolicy loader/governing runtime can change subsequent behaviour. Last integration is a configured approval/composition path, not proven autonomous promotion. - Boundary reachability: lib/daemonruntime/generalizer.py::actoncluster emits first-party rule proposals from harvested signals; execution/daemons/anthus/anthus.py invokes harvesting, and loader/gate read current policies. This is a prospective adaptation constructor path within the shipped system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A concrete option generator and policy return surface go beyond internal task planning; C, not A, because decisions on cross-cutting rule adoption remain operator-gated. - Evidence: general; anthus; agentload; policy - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Maintain the single-operator swarm&#39;s legitimate organizational policy and identity boundary under a named ultimate human principal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cross-agent role authority, rules for what may be done autonomously, and conflicts between proposed policy drift and operator-owned governing commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Approve or reject changes to ultimate agent policies, grant scope and identity/action authority, which later runtime decisions must obey. - Decision owner: One explicitly documented founder/operator principal governs the single-operator reference. Agent-generated rule proposals cannot overwrite operator-authored policies; multi-operator ownership/quorum remains design-only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Neotoma-canonical agentpolicy, signed agent-to-principal identity and grants, operator-only blast class and runtime fail-closed policy reader, change proposals. - Closure path: Agent observes/presents proposed policy/authority revision → legitimate operator approves/adopts into canonical Neotoma policy → runtime loads resulting approved policy and applies it to subsequent agent dispatch/action gating. Human approval of individual routine PRs alone is NOT the S5 witness. - Boundary reachability: The repo ships the single-principal regime and reads effective policies in gating.loadpolicy/agent loader; generalizer explicitly defers operator-conflicting and high-layer changes to the parent. The authority binding is part of the supported P1 reference, not the hypothetical P2–P5 distributed system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent may propose; ultimate identity/policy remains with the human principal and the authorized version returns into action constraints. No autonomous S5 mode is claimed. - Evidence: readme; policy; general; agentload; status - Basis: explicit and structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/907b625f88397c09eddfe65ec1374701d39e117d/assessments/at/ateles.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 907b625f88397c09eddfe65ec1374701d39e117d\">907b625 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T16:19:17Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gagans23/agent-os\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-os</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gagans23/agent-os/tree/d318787db1fce4163e470ac1c94f5a74d667303f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d318787db1fce4163e470ac1c94f5a74d667303f\">d318787 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user-submitted personal-agent tasks into model-generated operational results under persistent context and reusable skills. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, changing remembered/contextual information, matched skill procedures, provider responses, task failures and task-specific observations captured in the job trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generate the substantive task response/action content from the current command plus runtime-supplied memory/skill context. - Decision owner: the configured autonomous model actor reached through the first-party provider-backed agentfn mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CommandRouter, runjob(), provider adapters, persistent memory/jobs, hooks, skill matching, traces, risk classification and configured provider transport. - Closure path: command → router/risk admission → memory and matched-skill context → provider-backed autonomous agent function → result/trace persistence → returned user result and later memory/learning availability. - Boundary reachability: a configured provider is an explicitly supported standard operating mode; CommandRouter automatically converts it to agentfn and /run calls the first-party runjob() path. No adjacent development/evaluation agent is needed to supply the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party Python code transports context and invokes the model, but the substantive response for an admitted task is selected by the autonomous model actor rather than predetermined by the router. The model service itself remains an external dependency, as with other model-backed harnesses. - Evidence: pinned agentos/providers.py, agentos/commandrouter.py, agentos/runner.py and README provider/run documentation. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: with no provider configured agent-os can remain in deterministic fallback mode; A records the supported first-party model-backed operating mode, not a claim that every configuration is agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: suppress a concrete shared-state collision among simultaneously executing swarm sub-jobs so sibling operations can proceed without corrupting or blocking one another during shared-store initialization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent workers opening the shared jobs/memory SQLite stores can race on the journal-mode/WAL transition and produce database is locked failures before useful parallel work begins. - Decisive decision or feedback right: establish the shared database schema/WAL state once before fan-out rather than letting sibling workers race to establish it independently. - Decision owner: the first-party deterministic Orchestrator implementation/developer-selected coordination policy; no autonomous agent is shown choosing or revising this conflict-response rule at runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: initshareddb(), SQLite WAL/busy-timeout behavior, ThreadPoolExecutor, per-worker JobStore/AgentMemory connections and the normal swarm execution path. - Closure path: swarm admitted → initshareddb() initializes the shared stores → parallel worker connections open against already-prepared stores → sibling sub-jobs execute and return results instead of colliding on journal initialization. - Boundary reachability: initshareddb() is wired directly and unconditionally into first-party Orchestrator.run() before ThreadPoolExecutor fan-out in the shipped /swarm path; no downstream composition is needed to expose the coordination primitive. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2 function and feedback path are concrete, but the decisive response to the inter-worker conflict is a fixed runtime policy. The model may choose sub-tasks, but it does not own this coordination discretion, so the autonomous A threshold is not met. - Evidence: pinned agentos/orchestrator.py, especially initshareddb() and the explicit implementation comment explaining the database is locked race it prevents. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this classification is not based on decomposition, parallelism or synthesis by themselves; it is based on the specific inter-S1 shared-store collision and the first-party attenuation path. C records a real S2-specific first-party path without autonomous ownership of the decisive coordination rule. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently executing swarm sub-jobs, each performing a separate user-derived operational task through runjob() and producing its own result. - Inter-S1 disturbance: sibling workers contend on common jobs/memory SQLite initialization and can race on journal-mode switching, yielding database is locked. - Attenuating coordination relation: the orchestrator initializes/closes the common stores once before worker fan-out, leaving workers to open already-prepared WAL/schema state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after pre-initialization the sibling workers can enter their normal job execution paths concurrently rather than fail at shared-store setup. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the source explicitly ties the pre-fan-out relation to a concrete concurrency collision among sibling workers; it is not credited merely because a queue, shared database or parallel executor exists.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control function was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current job health, pending approvals, process failures, recent scores and swarm execution are observable or locally regulated, but no whole-installation resource/commitment/prioritization disturbance is shown under a qualifying S3 owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor with a whole-system current view and discretionary authority over shared current resources, commitments, priorities, constraints, accountability or synergy was established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /status, /health, job statistics, risk gates, human approvals for individual privileged commands, fixed supervisor restart/backoff rules, swarm concurrency configuration and persistent job state. - Closure path: no qualifying whole-system current-control decision→subsequent-operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the available machinery observes or enforces local/static decisions. A human can approve a particular privileged action, but that is not evidence of whole-system S3 parent governance. - Evidence: pinned agentos/commandrouter.py, approvals.py, supervisor.py, orchestrator.py, jobs/health surfaces and README governance documentation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: later versions or downstream operator organizations could add S3 without changing the meaning of this frozen assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no qualifying first-party complementary and sufficiently independent audit function was established inside agent-os. - Disturbance / variety regulated: traces, commands, approval decisions, scores and daily reliability summaries are recorded/checked, but they do not establish a separate first-party challenge channel to operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party auditor with materially independent access and a findings→control return path was found. - Decision owner: not established inside the assessed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace recording, hash-chained audit log and verify(), /audit, daily evaluation summaries, Ninja Harness invocation and persisted external scores. - Closure path: no qualifying first-party complementary audit finding is returned into current control as an independently owned S3 judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit database verifies integrity of the same recorded command/decision path; it does not independently challenge operational claims. Ninja Harness can supply a distinct evaluation judgment, but that judgment is owned by a separately scoped external system and is not imported into agent-os ownership. - Evidence: pinned agentos/audit.py, runner.py, dailyeval.py, README&#39;s explicit runtime/evaluation separation and batch-#89 boundary note. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external evaluation may still be operationally valuable and may feed adaptation proposals; the standalone VSM ownership boundary is narrower than an integrated multi-system deployment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: turn a demonstrated novel user workflow into a reusable future capability when a legitimate parent human accepts the proposed adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: successful external/user work can reveal recurring procedures not represented in the current skill library, creating future capability mismatch if the runtime cannot preserve and reuse the newly demonstrated workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether the proposed reusable SKILL.md becomes an installed capability for future agent runs. - Decision owner: the human/operator parent through the explicit /approve or /reject gate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace capture, success/novelty/complexity tests in proposeskill(), draft persistence, approval queue, skill-file write, SkillRegistry.reload() and later skill matching in runjob(). - Closure path: novel successful user task → first-party trace-derived skill draft → human review/approval → skill written into the live library and registry reloaded → later matching task receives the reusable procedure in its execution context. - Boundary reachability: the learning path is wired into ordinary first-party /run/approved-run completion through maybeproposeskill() and the same standard approval command surface; no custom extension or repository-development actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent-os autonomously constructs the candidate adaptation, but maintainer code explicitly refuses to install it silently. The decisive capability-change right belongs to the parent human, so the published state is standalone P, not A. - Evidence: pinned agentos/skillsynth.py, agentos/commandrouter.py, agentos/skillregistry.py, approvals.py, runner.py and README learning-loop documentation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the separate weak-run ImprovementProposal path is propose-only and requires an additional apply step; it is supporting evidence of conservative adaptation design, not an autonomous S4 closure. External Ninja scores may contribute a signal in some learning paths but do not own agent-os&#39;s parent adaptation decision. - External distinction: a real user/environment task succeeds through a procedure not already covered by the installed skill set; the learned option is grounded in the task&#39;s recorded operational trace rather than generated from an internal roadmap alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the draft is created specifically as a reusable skill so later matching work can exploit the demonstrated procedure instead of rediscovering it. - Adaptation option generated: a concrete SKILL.md draft containing name, triggers, reconstructed procedure and verification instructions. - Path back into current capability / S3: parent approval writes the draft to the active skills directory, reloads the registry, and subsequent runjob() calls can match/inject the new procedure into model context.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the declared installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider choice, risk rules, profiles, packs, skills and per-action approvals constrain behavior, but no evidenced identity/ultimate-policy dispute is escalated to and closed by a legitimate S5 authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying ultimate identity/policy decision path was established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured profiles/tool allowlists, pluggable risk classifier, human approval of privileged actions, provider configuration, role packs and static governance defaults. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → authority → authoritative decision → returned governing operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts/configuration and human vetoes over ordinary actions constrain S1 work but do not constitute S5 under the Profile without an identity-level issue and ultimate-policy closure. - Evidence: pinned agentos/profiles.py, risk.py, approvals.py, provider/pack configuration, command router and README governance material. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator obviously retains ordinary ownership of a self-hosted installation; the Methodology does not infer S5=P from generic configuration authority or per-task approvals.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7d01d7323bb17fab796dd0efd2045bbfa1075563/assessments/gagans-agent-os.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7d01d7323bb17fab796dd0efd2045bbfa1075563\">7d01d73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aohp-os/aohp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AOHP</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aohp-os/aohp/tree/b8ab9e98a2bd51f7fbd01a95d3b45dca078c5884\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b8ab9e98a2bd51f7fbd01a95d3b45dca078c5884\">b8ab9e9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: enact user-requested service outcomes through model-driven agents operating across application/system capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous apps/interfaces, user intent, GUI/API/CLI state, cross-app information and task-specific environmental changes during execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the agent actor chooses task actions/service-composition steps under the user&#39;s intent. - Decision owner: autonomous AI agent actor hosted by the AOHP harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OS APIs/CLIs/GUI access, background execution, memory, sandboxed sensitive values and information-flow tracking. - Closure path: agent choices invoke OS/application actions, observations return through the harness and subsequent agent behaviour responds until the requested service outcome is produced. - Why agent-owned: AOHP explicitly places agents as first-class operating actors; deterministic OS mechanisms execute/constrain rather than replace the agent&#39;s task decisions. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - AOHP supports parallel background interaction and shared OS resources, but the pinned repository evidence does not identify distinct S1 units plus a specific inter-S1 interference/oscillation and a corresponding coordination feedback path. OS scheduling, sandboxing and information-flow enforcement are not promoted merely because they can constrain concurrent actors. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The OS can enforce permissions, information-flow constraints, background execution and user-consent gates, but no distinct first-party actor is evidenced with a whole-system current view plus discretionary authority over shared operational resources, commitments or priorities. - Sensitive-transfer/payment consent is an action-level authorization mechanism; it does not establish a whole-system S3 parent loop. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - Security-oriented test cases and information-flow metadata validate/enforce the product&#39;s security model, but no independent runtime audit actor/path is shown challenging ordinary S1/S3 reporting and returning corrective findings into current control. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Personalization and cross-app memory can improve task context, but the pinned boundary does not establish an externally and prospectively oriented intelligence loop that models future environmental change, develops adaptation options and returns them into current organizational capability. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - User intent and consent are important authority inputs, and AOHP&#39;s security policies constrain execution. However, the cited paths concern task/sensitive-action authorization rather than an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue reaching legitimate parent authority and returning to govern the organization as a whole. - Static/security enforcement is therefore not promoted to S5 or P. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/aohp.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:59:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zwpdbh/octopus-in-rust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Octopus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zwpdbh/octopus-in-rust/tree/88695f8e9209c76522d5943a2207751fd382dd43\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 88695f8e9209c76522d5943a2207751fd382dd43\">88695f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue user-specified coding outcomes and focused subagent investigations/edits using model-chosen tools and first-party execution-feedback loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository uncertainty, failing tools, changing source/workspace state, subtask results, interruptions, provider and permission errors, and needed plan adjustments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root or child model selects the next code-reading, editing, command, plan, search or delegated task and adapts after actual returned result. - Decision owner: first-party octopus-cli KimiSoul model and child Agent sessions; the external model provider performs inference but native Brain and agent tools own the workflow and action routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent-core streaming Brain, KimiSoul toolset, tool registry, prompt profiles, hooks, permissions, checkpoints, background task manager and child status store. - Closure path: user task → native coding-agent model/tool call → approval and real tool/subagent execution → observation or child report → same model&#39;s next coding decision or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/agent.yaml registers the native Agent tool; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs creates and runs named child sessions with own model/tool loop, while crates/agent-core/src/core/brain.rs implements executable streamed tool/result turns for the native root runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without model-selected actions and interpretation of tool/subagent outcomes, file/LLM transports and status stores cannot choose what work to do next. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/agent.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/kimisoul.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/brainbridge.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs; crates/agent-core/src/core/brain.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/agent.yaml. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this demonstrates first-party operational model rights, not success in any live programming benchmark or all model settings.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no implemented native inter-S1 interference/oscillation attenuation relation is demonstrated for the concurrently runnable coding subagents at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlapping parent/child or child/child actions could contend for the same files, task responsibility or tool permissions; the source provides child launch/status and user-approved tool execution but not collision-specific conflict detection and feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no established native mutually-exclusive work claim, inter-agent dependency arbitration, shared write conflict adjudication or returned attenuation to conflicting S1s. Approval is about user permission for each requested tool action, not collision between peer commitments. - Decision owner: root model for delegation and standard permission/runtime for operation gating; no observed distinct S2 coordination decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LaborMarket for task role selection, SubagentStore for running/completed/error status, shared approval callback with source IDs, background shell task controls and notification broker. - Closure path: a delegated child runs its own task and may complete asynchronously; shared registry and approvals track each task, but no conflict-specific outcome is returned to multiple S1s to attenuate their interference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrent spawning and monitoring is not itself collision arbitration; agent roles, status messages, and one approval queue do not demonstrate cross-agent conflict regulation. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/subagents/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/approvalruntime/runtime.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/background/manager.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/background/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/notifications/manager.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: native background child tasks can genuinely run in parallel; their concurrency is acknowledged rather than denied. The negative is the absence of a demonstrated collision-specific coordination relation, not absence of multiple active agents.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current organization management owner with an evidenced global resource/commitment judgment returned to several simultaneously operating S1s was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task decomposition, background children and tool failures are handled as the root coding request. A list of running subagents exists but no first-party portfolio status→reassignment/control loop spanning current commitments was demonstrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root chooses to spawn a requested child, tool approvals constrain execution and subagent records can be queried in code; neither establishes whole-current reprioritization of a team/work portfolio. - Decision owner: root coding agent for task delegation and human user for tool consent, not an evidenced S3 controller of all current operative units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed Agent tool and task/result store, per-child approval source and session, local background task manager, notifications, prompt role policies and UI event streams. - Closure path: child tool results may return to the root for further coding, or background completion is recorded/logged; no whole-current status view with an owned global management decision and returned commitment change to multiple active workers is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing individual delegates and checking status is operational orchestration for the current request. A native parent model would need a demonstrated distinct global/current management right and closed corrective return to justify S3, not just tool access to async children. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/subagents/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/agent.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/notifications/manager.rs; crates/agent-core/src/control.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: other standalone bot/web service deployments may have their own operational oversight; those different recursions are not imported as terminal coding S3 owners.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary audit with sufficiently independent raw-evidence access, separate judgment and consequential return to present organizational control was demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code may be wrong, shell may fail, model summaries may be overconfident; standard tool results, build CI or self-requested exploration may expose errors but are routine task feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate audit selector/mandate distinct from the producing model and current task role that probes alternative primary evidence and makes an accountable audit judgment for current management. - Decision owner: root or child executing tasks, user for approval and maintainer CI for external development checks; no native S3 auditor/manager identified at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: file/command result logs, hooks on tool use/failure, status notifications, explore role with read-only prompt policy, checkpoint/rollback DMail, developer workflows. - Closure path: tool outcomes, hook events and reports return to the same task loop or user; no independent adversarial audit → findings → distinct current control decision → corrected operational follow-up is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model with read-only tools or separate session can perform code search, but a generic explore subtask and hook are not independently commissioned risk-based audit/alternate truth with returned S3 authority. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/agent.yaml; apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/explore.yaml; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/hooks/engine.rs; crates/agent-core/src/hooks/policy.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/brainbridge.rs; .github/workflows/ci.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user might compose an external auditor using generic tools, but no specific shipped independent complementary audit mode is shown here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective external environment modelling with adaptation options returned into present managerial capability choices was established at the coding-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider errors, new user tasks, changing source files, plugin availability, background task events and prompt context are operational disturbances, not forecast-driven future capability management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: runtime retries/recovery rules and model-guided coding/plans govern current operations; no future-facing option generator and S4→S3 returned tradeoff decision is implemented. - Decision owner: current operative agent for task steps; developer/user for plugin/provider/tool configurations, not an evidenced native S4 intelligence authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retry/backoff, recovery policy, compaction, prompt injection, plugin discovery, session state and task planning. - Closure path: new tool or source information informs current model context and retries; no externally prospective sensing → tested adaptation alternatives → current organization management conversation → new capability commitment cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, planning, checkpoint rollback and newly available plugins aid current task execution but do not meet the future-oriented organizational adaptation criterion. - Evidence: crates/agent-core/src/core/retry.rs; crates/agent-core/src/core/recovery.rs; crates/agent-core/src/session/compaction.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/dynamicinjection.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/plugin/discovery.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/plan/mod.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the same monorepo hosts external-facing applications with their own changing market/user contexts; they are separate from the specified installed coding agent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party highest-level identity/ultimate-policy decision authority and returned binding governance path is established for this terminal coding agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool execution safety, human approvals, read-only exploration, model settings and plugin permissions concern immediate technical boundaries, not competing identity/mission claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users approve/deny particular operations, runtime applies approved/denied tool modes; maintainer AGENTS.md defines contribution rules but not executable legitimate first-party ultimate-policy arbitration. - Decision owner: operator for local consent and developer for static tool policies, not a verified S5 actor resolving current/future whole-system identity conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool approval, per-subagent approval source/queue, default role tool policy, security hooks and provider configurations. - Closure path: unsafe tool request is denied or allowed, which affects the current task; no durable supreme identity/ethos decision returns to S3/S4 governance of subsequent operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary human permission decisions or source policy text do not create a genuine highest-authority organizational policy loop. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/approvalruntime/runtime.rs; crates/agent-core/src/core/approval.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/approval.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/agent.yaml; AGENTS.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: user organization, project maintainers or app-specific human policies may exercise legitimate governance externally, not as first-party native S5 for this coding agent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3f308b59dc96279cae7272e505c0bd705aec00ec/assessments/octopus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3f308b59dc96279cae7272e505c0bd705aec00ec\">3f308b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alibaba/open-code-review\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCodeReview</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alibaba/open-code-review/tree/7a571b78d3493b249f6ad14d835c6a79a0a67d2e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7a571b78d3493b249f6ad14d835c6a79a0a67d2e\">7a571b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: inspect assigned code/diff material and produce substantive review findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: language/framework variation, repository context, defect/risk ambiguity, cross-file evidence and changing source content. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the review agent decides which potential issues warrant comments and which context/tools to use before finishing its subtask. - Decision owner: model-driven OCR-managed review agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic file selection, bundling, rules, tool registry, line anchoring and comment worker pool. - Closure path: agent inspection/tool results change subsequent reasoning; accepted codecomment results become the delivered review output. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - Multiple file-group subagents can run with isolated contexts and concurrency, but the pinned evidence does not establish a specific mutual interference/conflict/oscillation among operational S1 units plus a feedback relation that regulates it. Divide-and-conquer bundling and asynchronous scheduling alone are not S2. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - Deterministic orchestration controls selection, grouping, rule application and scheduling, but no distinct autonomous actor has both a current whole-review view and discretionary authority over shared organizational resources, commitments or priorities. Pipeline control is not promoted to S3. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - The reflection/positioning machinery is separate in implementation but runs as part of the ordinary codecomment production/post-processing path. The reviewed evidence does not reconstruct a sufficiently independent complementary access path that challenges normal operational reporting and returns findings to a distinct current-control owner. - Put differently, “independent module” is not sufficient by itself: reflection here is routine quality processing of the review product, and no separate corrective organizational loop is established at this boundary. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Repository search, dynamic context retrieval and production-derived tool tuning improve review execution but do not form an external-and-prospective environmental intelligence loop with adaptation options returned into current organizational capability. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Review rules, configured models/providers and operator-selected execution modes constrain the product, but no runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority and return loop is established. Human consumption/disposition of review comments is outside the supplied harness governance path. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/open-code-review.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T13:42:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/p0systems/peezy-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Peezy</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/p0systems/peezy-cli/tree/4abb65bb3e8c9f6c4cdd42c22d29b61531bffad3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4abb65bb3e8c9f6c4cdd42c22d29b61531bffad3\">4abb65b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify a software project using model-selected coding/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository state, implementation choices, tool/process failures, context pressure, provider behavior and denied actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret their outcomes, adapt after failures/denials and decide when the task is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Peezy agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, gateway streaming, approvals, path confinement, todo state, compaction, session persistence/resume, MCP registration and interrupt handling. - Closure path: user objective → model selects tool/coding action → runtime executes or returns denial/error → result re-enters model history → model revises work → final answer closes the turn. - Boundary reachability: both interactive and headless entry points construct the same first-party Agent and tool registry; headless changes approval handling without replacing the core decision loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code enforces permissions and transports tool results, but the model owns the open-ended coding decisions and evidence interpretation. - Evidence: README.md; src/core/agent.ts; src/core/create-agent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ask/plan modes can constrain execution, but that does not transfer the coding decision itself away from the model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material inter-S1 coordination function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none at the declared recursion because the standard runtime does not instantiate distinct concurrent operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: todo sequencing, MCP tool registration and tool execution order remain inside one S1 loop. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic task ordering/tool extensibility is not peer interference attenuation. - Evidence: src/core/agent.ts; src/core/tools/todo.ts; src/core/mcp/manager.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: connected MCP servers may themselves contain agents, but external systems cannot donate S2 to Peezy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no portfolio of distinct current operational commitments exists under a separate controller. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval prompts, request caps, abort signals, plan-mode mutation rejection and todo visibility constrain one S1 turn. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human approval is action-local permission and interrupt is termination enforcement; neither provides a whole-system current view plus substantive control over resources/commitments. - Evidence: src/core/agent.ts; src/core/tools/safety.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic approval or stop controls are explicitly insufficient for S3 under Methodology 0.3.6.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no separate actor independently challenges an operational claim using a complementary evidence path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the same coding agent may run tests, inspect diffs or use read tools; plan mode and todo tracking do not create independent review. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no fresh reviewer/verifier role or separate evidence channel is wired into the standard distribution. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/system.ts; src/core/agent.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can ask the same agent or an external MCP tool to review; generic composition is not a first-party independent audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no future/external distinction is transformed into an adaptation option that changes current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session resume, compaction, provider/model configuration, live MCP changes and project instructions preserve/reconfigure context but do not themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: compaction summarizes prior context and configuration selects tools/models; neither is an autonomous prospective adaptation owner. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/compaction.ts; src/core/session/store.ts; src/core/create-agent.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: persistent experience can inform later S1 turns, but persistence/context reuse is insufficient S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level policy matter is routed to an authoritative owner and returned as governing runtime policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compiled system prompt, approval modes, path confinement, project instructions and provider/tool configuration constrain operations. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Peezy operates within user/developer-authored rules and has no authority to redefine its identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/system.ts; src/core/agent.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: action approvals are not identity governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1990b5aa53ad357c8c87753b6ceaccd697711001/assessments/peezy.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1990b5aa53ad357c8c87753b6ceaccd697711001\">1990b5a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T21:55:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zap-coding-agent/zap-coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zap</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zap-coding-agent/zap-coding-agent/tree/9812a142b1d12c894c98a6f5d08cc776ecc760da\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9812a142b1d12c894c98a6f5d08cc776ecc760da\">9812a14 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose and carry out codebase engineering work under uncertainty, including source inspection, modifications, shell/test execution and error-driven repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown program structure and test behavior, failed commands, tool limitations, insufficient context and unexpected code interactions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model selects the next specific tool/argument or correction after observing actual results; invoked child runs its own independent model-tool loop. - Decision owner: active coding model in Session (or in a delegated child), not shell/tool dispatcher, task-index or watchdog. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM client, session context and turn loop, code index, file/shell tools, permission gate, turn and verification budgets. - Closure path: user goal → model-selected read/edit/execute action → source or shell result → next model turn or completion; tool failure may trigger model-directed revision. - Boundary reachability: normal CLI/TUI session and first-party spawnagent registered in the default non-child session runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same deterministic tools alone cannot invent appropriate engineering actions without a model decision. - Evidence: README.md; ARCHITECTURE.md; turn.rs; tools.rs; mod.rs; agent.rs; agentcore.rs - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no specific observed or source-wired first-party attenuation of material inter-S1 interference with returned behavior change is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent write-capable delegated children can collide in a shared working tree; duplicative work and resource contention are plausible inter-worker disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no inspected controller detects such a concrete collision and limits or reallocates participating child work; source tool&#39;s filesinscope is not consumed by the dispatch path. - Decision owner: parent model verbally instructed to split independent work, with deterministic tool batching; neither is an executed S2 conflict resolution owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent prompts, optional filesinscope declaration, per-child session isolation, tool call parallelism, user-operated background session tracker. - Closure path: child task may return a result to the parent; no independently evidenced conflict report → attenuation affecting the conflicting children → updated child work closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a concurrent spawnagent response and “do not edit same file” instruction do not detect and settle actual inter-S1 interference, and UI background status is not a corrective channel. - Evidence: agent.rs; agentcore.rs; tools.rs; backgroundagent.rs; mod.rs; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural and explicit execute argument usage -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately evidenced current whole-organization managerial decision for the live roster/commitments of subordinate coding S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing delegated work, conflicting team priorities and resource/commitment tradeoffs of several simultaneous coding workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent can issue additional isolated child subtasks and consume their final answers; no built-in whole-current roster view and discretionary active priority reassignment applied to existing model-running child tasks was found. - Decision owner: parent coding model controls its present task decomposition, while /bg list/start/kill remains a user operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-session task lists, user background agent status panel, automatic model routing by task classifier, plan step runner and tool watchdog. - Closure path: delegated output returns to parent coding turn, and user background status to UI; no documented active whole-team status → discretionary control choice → changed current commitments across children and closed return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decomposition of one coding request into independent calls, or one process&#39;s retries/verification, is S1 strategy and task execution rather than whole-current organizational control. - Evidence: agent.rs; backgroundagent.rs; mod.rs; taskplanner.rs; planexecution.rs; watchdog.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separately owned independent source-first auditor forms a corrective challenge to a coding worker&#39;s claims and returns to a distinct current-control manager within the shipped organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent&#39;s potentially false edit/test-success claims, missed regressions or unsafe modifications. - Decisive decision or feedback right: watchdog detects repeated failing verification commands, and plans require tests, but the same coding loop acts on those outputs; no separately commissioned independent auditor verdict and managerial corrective return is wired as a first-party mode. - Decision owner: normal code verification remains with originating coding model and deterministic watchdog; human/CI reviewers are outside runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: watchdog.rs per-command fail ledger/nudges and tool withdrawal, planexecution.rs step verification rules, shell output, user-visible audit log. - Closure path: test failure returns to the same coding actor and can alter its next step or cause stop; no independent reviewer → distinct manager → affected coder corrective cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: watchdog and editing-plan QA are task execution reliability protections, not separate complementary audit ownership. - Evidence: watchdog.rs; planexecution.rs; tools.rs; turn.rs; agent.rs; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no agentic outside/future-oriented organizational capability strategy plus applied capacity adaptation cycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing coding requirements, future language support, provider/skill constraints and environment capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate actor selects long-horizon capability changes from external signals and implements them into the agent organization. - Decision owner: developer/operator configures provider, skill/index settings and deployments; model addresses the current coding task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AST code-index, dynamic skill matching and context refresh, task planning, persistence and provider routing. - Closure path: contexts or skills inform current action, but no future organizational-capability choice → decision → implementation → feedback loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive context retrieval and task-level planning are not equivalent to future-capacity intelligence. - Evidence: mod.rs; skillmanager.rs; memoryrefresh.rs; taskplanner.rs; mod.rs; contextmanager.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate-purpose/policy authority over the agent organization is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mission/identity tradeoffs distinct from per-command safety decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5 owner arbitrates organizational purpose and returns binding governing change to multiple S1s. - Decision owner: user/operator and developer configuration set purpose/permissions; built-in permission gate and watchdog enforce narrower runtime safety conditions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permissions, destructive-command checks, sandbox, task goals, user config and runtime tool policy. - Closure path: allow/deny a shell or file operation, nudge/stop a repeatedly failing agent and return technical status; no higher-purpose identity decision or constitutional binding return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety policy enforcement does not assume ownership of the organization&#39;s ultimate identity or mission. - Evidence: permissionmanager.rs; tools.rs; watchdog.rs; mod.rs; mod.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dcee04ec283e9c0cc14102fea8ce86b587e3203a/assessments/zap.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dcee04ec283e9c0cc14102fea8ce86b587e3203a\">dcee04e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T23:49:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Active Graph</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/tree/50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c\">50d8405 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute context-sensitive agent steps against a changing graph/goal, using model-selected responses, tools and observed results in the supported live-provider mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New goal types, variable evidence and graph events, differing tool result content, tool/model failures and uncertain model next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Registered @llmbehavior provider model selects a response or declared tool call from the current graph view, tool definitions and prior tool messages; output handler turns model results into task-relevant graph mutations and reactive follow-up. - Decision owner: Configured LLM through first-party LLMBehavior, Runtime.invokellm, LLMProvider.complete and tool dispatch; the provider infrastructure is external, but the shipped model-decision composition is implemented here. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event subscriptions, contextual graph view, frame, policy, tools registry, behavior scheduling, accepted patches, recorded event causality, tool-turn bounds and tool-error feedback. - Closure path: Goal/event → model-backed behavior → provider completion → selected tool(s) executed → result messages re-enter same model loop → final interpreted output and proposed graph patch → event-sourced graph updated → next event triggers model/deterministic behavior. - Boundary reachability: Public @llmbehavior and Runtime(graph,llmprovider=...) directly enter runtime-owned invokellm → live provider call/replay → tool dispatch → result to subsequent LLM turn → BehaviorGraph proposal. The bundled Diligence pack includes executable model-backed behavior definitions; the offline quickstart exercises identical event/behavior composition with a deliberately recorded surrogate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: If the model actor is removed but the event router, graph store, tool registry and policy remain, deterministic subscribers can fire but cannot generate varied task-sensitive decisions and tools from unknown evidence. Recorded fixtures are not live cognition; opt-in shipped provider mode is the S1 positive. - Evidence: activegraph/behaviors/decorators.py#L215, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1050, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1293, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1481, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1903, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1979, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L2097, activegraph/packs/diligence/behaviors.py#L111. - Basis: Structural first-party model-provider/tool feedback route with supported optional live mode, plus actual recorded-fixture offline quickstart. - Confidence: High for source reachability and offline execution; model service reliability unverified. - Caveats: Optional external provider SDK/API is needed for real LLM decisions; offline fixture success verifies the orchestrating mechanics only, not model performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct first-party owner of regulation of specific disturbances between independently viable S1 operating divisions with mutually revised commitments is evidenced at the declared generic graph-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent graph patches, relation-edge changes, conflicting object updates and event-trigger order can interfere with graph consistency; those disturbances are addressed by optimistic consistency/rejection and queued deterministic handling, not an evidenced portfolio of separately viable S1 operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Registered relation behaviors and graph-event selectors apply user-authored transformations; runtime queues, patch conflict rejection and serialized updates resolve graph state validity. No source-backed independent S1 units plus separate feedback-regulating owner are specified. - Decision owner: Developer-defined relation/regular behavior code and deterministic runtime patch/queue rules; any optional model-backed relation would need concrete independent-S1 ownership evidence beyond this general API. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed relations and reactive handlers, event subscription/patterns, optimistic patch validation, deterministic event queue, bounded scheduling and runtime commit log. - Closure path: Event/patch contention → deterministic commit/reject → subsequent event handlers read revised graph; this is shared-state coherence, not inter-S1 operational interference → S2 decision → adjusted independent organizational commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing a hypothetical coordinating model still leaves the same generic queue/patch-rejection machinery. A programmable relation handler is a possible application construction site, not a proven default S2 autonomously exercising relational coordination. - Evidence: activegraph/behaviors/decorators.py#L150, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1072, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1293, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L103, activegraph/core/patch.py#L1, activegraph/core/graph.py#L1. - Basis: Bounded negative in first-party runtime and bundled example, not universal claim about all third-party packs. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separate application may configure real independent S1 divisions and bind a model-led relation coordinator, requiring a new system boundary and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party autonomous whole-current manager controls independent viable operating divisions&#39; resources, priorities and obligations with a managerial return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Event queue or LLM/tool budget exhaustion, failed behaviors, sandbox constraints and approval waiting can threaten a graph run, but these are local run-level risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer supplies Frame, Policy, budget limits, tools and registered behaviors; first-party executor checks them, schedules ready behaviors and emits failure/budget events. No separate agent is shown renegotiating a portfolio of independent S1 commitments. - Decision owner: Human/application constructor and deterministic runtime budget/scheduler/authority evaluator, not an operational S3 executive. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frame goal/constraints, budget consumption and runquantum, scheduler, policy capability ceilings, approval and failure events, event sink status. - Closure path: Run condition → hard-coded/configured guard or scheduling choice → current behavior invoked/stopped/failure logged; missing whole-current viability manager → new binding priorities/resource rights returned to multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Budget guards and queues are safety/throughput controls; removing a hypothetical model S3 still leaves all their fixed effects intact. - Evidence: activegraph/frame.py#L1, activegraph/policy.py#L1, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L55, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1072, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1364. - Basis: Bounded first-party negative including provider-configured model behavior, deterministic and pack modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Downstream application may manage many viable S1 systems atop this runtime; generic registered behavior count is not itself that scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate first-party complementary reality-inspection function independently challenges a current S3 manager&#39;s operating picture and returns a corrective action to that manager. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete causal traces, untrusted tool/model outputs and behavior faults can mislead users. Event logs, strict replay, fork/diff and observability expose inconsistencies but do not themselves close managerial audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Replay divergence checks and error events can flag mismatched execution; an operator chooses what to inspect. No distinct S3 agent samples actual S1 reality, judges S3 reports and sends an authorized correction into separate S3 decision rights. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic trace/replay machinery and outside human/developer for interpretation; no autonomous audit owner at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Append-only event log, causal provenance, strict replay, tracing/metrics/sinks, patch rejection events and fork/diff. - Closure path: Events → trace or replay discrepancy → user inspection/failure event; no independent operational audit finding → established S3 receives it → changes current S1 portfolio control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Full replayability is valuable evidence infrastructure but removing a putative auditor does not change the deterministic event-history artifact. Evidence capture is not an independent audit decision/closure by itself. - Evidence: activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3393, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3583, activegraph/runtime/diff.py#L108, activegraph/trace/causal.py#L1, activegraph/observability/status.py#L1. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking dedicated observability and strict replay capabilities. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Users may implement a qualified external reviewer or application-specific reality-checking model; no such legitimate S3-to-S3 relation is bundled.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party model-owned future environment distinction → prospective changed strategy option → authorized durable adoption back into later operations cycle is established by the shipped event-sourcing/replay/fork mechanisms. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/prompt/graph configuration hypotheses, possible failure recurrence and drift can be explored with forks; the choices of what to test and retain remain application/developer-owned. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runtime.fork creates a branch from recorded history, diff compares structural outcomes, and replay cache preserves event/model responses. The caller determines alternative behaviors/policies and whether/how to deploy them; no packaged model evaluates environmental future shifts and autonomously updates durable operating capabilities. - Decision owner: Outside experimenter/developer for prospective configuration; deterministic runtime for producing comparable fork traces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Replay cache, fork IDs, Diff computation, consistent event roots, saved packs/behaviors and optional user-supplied configuration. - Closure path: Operator forks run → changes input configuration → replay and diff → operator interprets result; no internal model-generated prospective strategy with authorized adoption into next live baseline operating mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A future developer can use the instrumentation for an S4 experiment, but removing any supposed strategic model does not disable graph replay, fork or diff; no model-owned future option-generation-and-adoption path is first-party evidenced. - Evidence: activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3393, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3583, activegraph/runtime/diff.py#L79, activegraph/llm/cache.py#L1, README.md#L42. - Basis: Bounded negative even though deterministic experimentation infrastructure is strong. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared generic runtime boundary. - Caveats: A user-created LLM behavior might intentionally analyze trends and update a future skill; that is application-specific S4, not shipped default first-party ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate ultimate identity/purpose authority deciding constitutional conflicts with a lasting policy return is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool capabilities, graph mutation scope, replay security, sandbox access and human approvals bound model behavior but do not define organization-level identity decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Policy/frame ceilings and evaluateactionauthority enforce user-supplied permissions; explicit operator approval can control capabilities, but no internally accountable actor deliberates and sets final purpose for a viable organization. - Decision owner: External developer/operator supplies governance intent; first-party deterministic permission/approval enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AuthorityDecision, capability ceilings and policy configuration, patch validation, approval events, sandbox and budget locks. - Closure path: Tool/patch request → predetermined policy or outside approval → action allowed/denied; no identity-question escalation to legitimate S5 and durable return of constitutional policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The string Policy describes an enforceable rule set, not organizational legitimacy or the right to decide ultimate purpose. - Evidence: activegraph/policy.py#L1, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L55, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L103, activegraph/frame.py#L1, README.md#L150. - Basis: Bounded negative after first-party frame/authority and human approval modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The human/application owning a higher-level organization may exercise S5 externally; not claimed inside this runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3f3f65314f63d2b23f5de0b1c2512d780daee008/assessments/ac/active-graph.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3f3f65314f63d2b23f5de0b1c2512d780daee008\">3f3f653 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T17:45:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hash-7777/HashCortX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HashCortX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hash-7777/HashCortX/tree/ab3fb74a61928dfb98985eac5ec3eeac8c85afe7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ab3fb74a61928dfb98985eac5ec3eeac8c85afe7\">ab3fb74 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A single autonomous model-directed coding loop acts on user project files and tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code, failing commands, requested edits and tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select files/commands/edits, observe first-party tool results and determine the next coding action. - Decision owner: Main model-driven HashCoder agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Native Tauri/Rust tool adapters, permission gates, model routing, session persistence, project scope and diff/undo. - Closure path: User task -&gt; model-chosen coding tool -&gt; first-party handler and tool feedback -&gt; new model choice or final result. - Boundary reachability: HashCoder&#39;s shipped code mode calls its native agentLoop and Tauri tool definitions on a real opened project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses actions; the program executes and records those choices. - Evidence: mode.js; hashcoder.js; agent-policy.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Inference comes from configured external models; permissions and human approval can constrain actions..\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No established first-party inter-S1 regulation inside the HashCoder coding runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A single coding agent and its tool batch do not form distinct operational units in conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No S2-specific choice. - Decision owner: No S2 decision owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Concurrent read batching, sequential writes, session and UI state. - Closure path: Tools are grouped and executed for the same operational agent, not corrective coordination among independent S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parallel read tools and sequential writes only implement one agent&#39;s tool ordering; the separate Swarm workspace is excluded. - Evidence: agent-policy.js; mode.js; graph.js; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Sibling Swarm team design is an adjacent system and cannot donate its decisions to HashCoder..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-system current management over multiple coding operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Iteration stalls, limits and tool permission issues are local operating disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None for organizational resource/commitment authority above one coding S1. - Decision owner: No qualifying S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stall detector, step budgets, runtime gate, provider fallback, status and trace. - Closure path: Stalled or unproven work is returned to the same coding agent or stopped; no whole-current cross-unit regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic current action limits are enforcement mechanisms, not a metasystem choosing shared unit priorities. - Evidence: agent-policy.js; mode.js; verify.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: These protections may improve performance/safety but do not establish organizational S3..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent audit role with complementary evidence access and corrective organizational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding agent may prematurely claim correct changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Proof tracker evaluates recorded test commands and deterministic send-back conditions, not independent review judgment. - Decision owner: Main agent retains correction ownership; proof checker applies static record checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project test command discovery, proofLog, change timestamps and bounded completion prompts. - Closure path: Recorded same-loop tool output -&gt; deterministic nudge -&gt; same model executes or answers; no separate auditor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The evidence is generated from ordinary agent operations; separate audit access/owner is missing. - Evidence: verify.js; mode.js; agent-policy.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Proof log can honestly describe which tests ran, without becoming S3..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective external-environment adaptation loop in the credited HashCoder mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future capability demands and external changes are not explicitly converted into adaptation options. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No evidenced S4 capability renewal right. - Decision owner: No S4 agent, constructor or parent owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Automatic user-fact extraction, memory recall, model choice, persisted state and tools. - Closure path: Facts may affect later prompts but no prospective environmental intelligence decision changes the current operating organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context retention and provider fallback are not prospective organizational adaptation. - Evidence: store.js; mode.js; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Other desktop workspaces are separately scoped and cannot close S4 here..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party organizational identity or ultimate-policy governance loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool permission/safety choices concern individual operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No ultimate-policy decision right at the selected recursion. - Decision owner: Human grants individual permissions; deterministic rules enforce. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission Guard, Rust deny list, user action dialog, diff Keep/Undo, audit log. - Closure path: User approves or denies an operation, without a binding identity/ultimate-policy decision loop returned to the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Command authorization and selected model are not S5-level ultimate purpose. - Evidence: hashcoder.js; agentsandbox.rs; SECURITY.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Runtime control is not inherited from broader OSS development governance..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4ed17d3bae037310dcd91af45f247ffb34cbe94e/assessments/hashcortx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4ed17d3bae037310dcd91af45f247ffb34cbe94e\">4ed17d3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:03:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nattergabriel/termcoder\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Termcoder</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nattergabriel/termcoder/tree/c642c36e8d65ca42a8ae05172c527f73cb2017d7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c642c36e8d65ca42a8ae05172c527f73cb2017d7\">c642c36 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out uncertain coding/developer tasks through chosen code-inspection, search, editing and shell operations informed by real tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown project structure, unavailable files, code defects, command failure, unexpected results and subsequent needed steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses a sequence of typed tools and the follow-up or terminal response based on previous tool outputs. - Decision owner: model-driven first-party Agent.runturn workflow. Provider supplies model inference but the installed app binds tools, executes actions and supplies feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry, provider adapter, State, tool implementations, permission callbacks, channel UI, config/skills, iteration cap and rollback. - Closure path: user task → model tool request → permission decision → real registered tool execution → ToolResult appended as model context → model chooses next action or completes turn. - Boundary reachability: app.build supplies the native registry/provider/permission callback to Agent, and the supported terminal/Telegram modes run that same first-party loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model choice leaves a deterministic tool list and permission prompts but no context-sensitive coding plan, inspection or completion decision. - Evidence: agent loop; app composition; tools; integration tests. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high for implementation-defined tool feedback; live provider-backed task not reproduced. - Caveats: mutating calls remain subject to human permission, and external providers remain dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no autonomous or constructor-owned first-party regulation of specific interference/oscillation between distinct operating S1 units is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple tool calls and incoming prompts are handled within one agent&#39;s own sequence, rather than competing independently goal-directed agent workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serial dispatchall runs model-requested tools; UI input queue transports future user messages without conflict arbitration between autonomous S1s. - Decision owner: model for its own operation, runtime for serial dispatch, channel for input handling; no separate S2 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, sequential dispatch, Telegram input queue, slash-command/user channel routing. - Closure path: result of each tool feeds the same model&#39;s next inference. No inter-S1 conflict signal → specific attenuation decision → changed subsequent worker obligations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing one operative&#39;s tools or swapping communications channels is not the S2 functional relation. - Evidence: loop.py; telegram.py; app.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within shipped single-agent modes. - Caveats: independent user-launched termcoder processes could interfere externally without creating native cross-process coordination.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current management function over a population of operating S1 units with authority over shared resources/commitments is instantiated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task and conversation progress are local to the only S1, not a managed organization&#39;s distinct current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coding model decides its own tool sequence; channel/user can approve or refuse operations; no whole-system manager allocates resources, changes priority across workers or returns corrected commitments. - Decision owner: the active coding model, human permissions/configuration and deterministic runtime; no separate S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iteration limit, provider/model commands, task-local message state, approval gate. - Closure path: task feedback and user guidance directly return to the operative model; no distinct whole-current view → corrective S3 decision → multiple S1 adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: present coding-task management is S1 even when the model can change models or adjust its next action. - Evidence: app.py; loop.py; model command; config. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for standard application boundary. - Caveats: external human operating several instances is not first-party returned S3 governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent reality-sampling auditor emits a separate judgment and closes a feedback path into S3 management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool/test failures can be seen by the active coding model and tests can be run as tools, but these are ordinary execution feedback rather than independent operational auditing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model may invoke tests/shell or view error messages; repo integration tests run by external developers/CI. None constitutes a first-party independent audit decision and management return. - Decision owner: operative coding model for tool-followup, developer/CI for separate quality tests; no S3 audit owner in the deployed agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell result, tool error marker, iterative model reasoning, developer tests. - Closure path: failed tool or test output → same agent&#39;s subsequent action; no independent complementary auditor → managerial owner → binding corrective instruction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routine self-verification and off-runtime test suites do not establish the S3 function. - Evidence: loop.py; Bash tool; loop test; development CI. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human review or a downstream plugin could introduce an audit, but no such shipped ownership loop was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party closed future/environment-sensing adaptation function is shipped to alter sustained organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider/models and source content may influence today&#39;s coding task; there is no prospective adaptation program. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user selects/configures provider, model and skills; a model may adapt local tactics after tool feedback, not authorize a future-oriented change in organizational capability. - Decision owner: user/developer for configuration and skill provision, current task model for immediate work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider registry, slash commands, global/project config, discovered Agent Skills and project instructions. - Closure path: user changes settings → next local session/tool choices; no distinct outside/future observation → capability proposal → S4 decision → returned organizational adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-local reasoning, model switching and skill activation are not substantive external-and-prospective S4 by themselves. - Evidence: config.py; app.py; SkillCatalog; provider command. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high on bounded standard modes. - Caveats: custom extensions might implement a separate adaptation function, not attributed to standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party legitimate ultimate-policy or continuing-identity arbitration loop at the agent-organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission postures, project instructions and credentials govern local tool execution, not organization identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human approval and static policy choose whether particular tool calls are allowed. No S5 identity decision is made and returned as binding whole-system governance. - Decision owner: user/configuration for permissions, deterministic runtime for enforcement; no S5 decisive owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: askeach, allowreadonly, allowall, project/user config and tool metadata. - Closure path: user/local config → permission callback → tool allowed/denied. No legitimate ultimate authority receives an S5 issue and returns an organizational identity-level decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a permission gate or a system prompt cannot be credited as ultimate policy/identity decision ownership without the function-specific closure. - Evidence: permissions.py; config.py; app.py; prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within shipped modes. - Caveats: project maintainer governance occurs outside the packaged agent&#39;s operational recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e953e47c78813c4c7796ae402f875b38e866a890/assessments/termcoder.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e953e47c78813c4c7796ae402f875b38e866a890\">e953e47 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T19:38:32Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Airavat-Research/cascaide-ts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cascaide-ts</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Airavat-Research/cascaide-ts/tree/08fc10fcd58ecf729b9b0c5c1f0c135f7bb723f7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 08fc10fcd58ecf729b9b0c5c1f0c135f7bb723f7\">08fc10f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform goal-directed agent work through repeated model decisions, tool/delegation actions and returned observations within a Cascaide cascade. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/application requests, evolving conversation history, model uncertainty, tool/sub-agent outputs and errors, provider responses and the local state needed to decide whether to act again or complete. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive answer, direct tool call, self-delegation or named-sub-agent delegation from the current model-visible context and incorporate returned results into the next turn. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent actor invoked through the first-party helper-built agent node. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: core node lifecycle, workflow context, provider adapters, tool-node execution, child-cascade spawning/waiting, recursion depth guard, streaming normalization, active-node tracking, optional persistence/hydration and client/server relay. - Closure path: cascade input/history → helper agent node → provider-backed model decision → answer or Tool/delegation call → first-party tool node executes the selected action/subcascade → tool result is appended to cascade history → helper respawns the agent node → model receives the observation and decides again or terminates. - Boundary reachability: @cascaide-ts/helpers is a shipped package explicitly documented as providing createReactAgent, createRecursiveReactAgent, createSupervisorAgent and createRecursiveSupervisorAgent; those generated nodes execute directly through the documented @cascaide-ts/core engine rather than through a repository-development example path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cascaide&#39;s deterministic graph executor transports, persists and bounds the loop, while the model actor chooses substantive next actions from the current history and declared capabilities. External providers supply inference but are directly invoked as the actor in the first-party operating path. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; serverWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts; packages/helpers/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the application chooses the model, system prompt, direct tools and optional sub-agent catalog. Those constructor inputs bound S1 but do not move the turn-level action decision out of the model actor once the standard helper runtime is executing.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination function is established at the declared cascade recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel or delegated S1 units could in principle overlap, conflict or oscillate, but the reviewed standard distribution does not identify a concrete sibling-interference mode and close a response specifically intended to damp it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegateToSubAgents() can spawn several child cascades and wait for them; recursive delegation is described for independent subtasks; activeNodes tracks concurrent work; persistence claims prevent duplicate execution of a node instance. - Closure path: no first-party disturbance-specific coordination judgment was found that returns a conflict/oscillation attenuation decision into subsequent behavior of multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model may choose subtasks or named sub-agents, but delegation and task decomposition are not S2 by themselves. spawn, waitUntil, active-node bookkeeping and node claiming transport/order execution without selecting a coordination response to a evidenced inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; controller.ts; serverPersistenceMiddleware.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream graph/node code could use the generic controller to implement a domain-specific coordination mechanism, but a universal extension surface is not itself an S2 constructor path without function-specific interference and closure evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-cascade current-control function with agent-owned authority is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current whole-cascade commitments, competing priorities, resource constraints or local-vs-global operational exceptions would require a metasystemic current view and intervention right, but the packaged agent paths do not provide that relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established over current resources, commitments, priorities or constraints for the cascade as a whole. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkflowState exposes context/active nodes/history/errors; CascadeController can read state, spawn nodes, wait and fork; the Supervisor model can delegate to configured sub-agents and receives their completed outputs as tool results; execution-time buffering can checkpoint remaining graph work. - Closure path: no packaged path was found in which an agent receives a whole-cascade current operational view, chooses a current-control intervention over the organization as a whole and returns that decision into runtime state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervisor delegation remains task allocation. The standard Supervisor receives completed child results, not a first-party live whole-system current-control view. Generic controller state/read/write methods are handed to node/tool implementation code and can support downstream specialization, but they do not define or own an S3-specific decision right. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; controller.ts; workflowSlice.ts; serverWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an application may deliberately expose controller state and intervention tools to a model or human manager, but that would be a separate downstream organization requiring its own evidence rather than an inherited S3 state for the reusable base runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operational claims could require evidence beyond ordinary agent output/history, but the standard runtime does not add an independent challenge path over those claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for independent audit judgment. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable context events, node history/errors, full audit trails described for durable mode, provider-stream canonicalization, cascade hydration and fork/time-travel. - Closure path: no first-party path was found from complementary evidence or independent challenge → audit finding → downstream corrective current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence and replay improve observability and reconstructability, but they record the ordinary execution path. Forking a prior state permits alternative execution but supplies no independent auditor, distinct evidence channel or corrective audit authority by itself. - Evidence: serverPersistenceMiddleware.ts; serverHydrationMiddleware.ts; persistence.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream applications can build reviewers or validators as ordinary graph nodes, but no packaged independent audit relation with complementary access and corrective closure is owned by the reusable runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-future adaptation function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external conditions, opportunities, threats or future capability needs are not modeled by a packaged prospective adaptation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for generating and selecting future-oriented adaptation options from external distinctions. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cascade history/context persistence, hydration, forking/time travel, recursive delegation and application-definable graph transitions. - Closure path: no first-party external/future sensing → adaptation-option generation → return into current capability/control path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: durable state and forkable histories concern preservation/replay of current or past execution. Recursive delegation and graph evolution during a task remain operational/current regulation unless evidence establishes prospective environmental modeling and capability adaptation. - Evidence: serverHydrationMiddleware.ts; controller.ts; workflowSlice.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the generic graph can host research/adaptation agents supplied by an application, but those are downstream semantics rather than a first-party S4 constructor relation in the reviewed base distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the cascade recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity-level conflicts or unresolved S3–S4 policy tensions have no packaged escalation/decision path in the reusable runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for legitimate ultimate-policy or identity decisions. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: application-provided systemPrompt, model/tool/sub-agent configuration, UI-node/HITL construction capability, provider filters and generic graph controls. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → decision → subsequent-operation governance path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and configuration constrain behavior but do not themselves exercise runtime identity authority. Human interaction can be implemented as a client/UI node, including in scaffolded applications, but generic task-level HITL does not establish S5 and the reusable base does not define an ultimate-policy escalation relation. - Evidence: packages/helpers/src/agent/types.ts; clientWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream application may define identity/policy-specific human or agent nodes. Such a specialization is a separate system-in-focus and cannot donate S5 to the reusable Cascaide constructor/runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1a87050bf4e179a45f42eaca88d2515082fdac2a/assessments/cascaide-ts.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1a87050bf4e179a45f42eaca88d2515082fdac2a\">1a87050 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T21:45:40Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skyblanket/swarm-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">swarm-code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skyblanket/swarm-code/tree/53007abac1d521e32c2508af5328412bd6bbb67b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 53007abac1d521e32c2508af5328412bd6bbb67b\">53007ab &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing coding work by interpreting a user/task objective, selecting repository/tool actions, executing them and revising later action from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation alternatives, shell/file/network/tool results, provider uncertainty, context pressure, tool failures and changing task evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific tool action and revise that choice from returned tool/model evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed swarm-code Agent in an interactive/headless parent or /flows child process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structured tool-call loop; ToolExecutor; ToolRegistry; permissions/guardrails; sessions/context compaction; skills/MCP; provider adapters; crash recovery; step bounds. - Closure path: current task/history → model selects tool/action → first-party executor performs/denies it → result is appended to history → same model actor chooses another action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: the shipped interactive and headless entrypoints instantiate this loop directly; /flows launches the same product binary as subordinate S1 processes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining the executor, sessions and tool registry leaves enforcement machinery but removes the open-ended task-specific decision of what coding action to take next. - Evidence: src/agent.sw; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: headless and dangerous actions remain constrained by first-party permission/hard-deny policy; those constraints do not replace the model&#39;s ordinary coding discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate runtime-resource interference among multiple simultaneously active subordinate swarm-code S1 processes by bounding the active population and feeding observed completion/capacity back into subsequent admission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a workflow phase may contain more full coding-agent commitments than should execute simultaneously; unbounded spawning competes for CPU, memory, model/provider capacity and other host resources. The implementation explicitly replaced behavior that could fork an entire large phase at once with bounded queued admission. - Distinct S1 units: every launched /flows task is a separate headless swarm-code subprocess running the product&#39;s complete Agent/model/tool loop, with its own prompt/model/process lifecycle. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrently running full agent processes consume the same host/provider resource envelope; excessive simultaneous execution creates resource contention/oversubscription even when their semantic tasks are independent. - Attenuating coordination relation: flowsmaxparallel() defines the active cap; launchquota() counts current running and queued tasks and computes free capacity; fillphaseslots() starts only the allowed queued subset; the render loop re-polls status and refills slots after child termination. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child process status is refreshed every polling cycle. A running child occupies a slot and prevents further queued S1 admission; once it becomes terminal, the observed free slot permits a later queued S1 to start. The relation therefore changes which S1 is active as a function of current inter-S1 resource occupancy. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive claim is not based on having several agents or a workflow queue. It is based on a concrete dynamic attenuation path over simultaneous S1 resource demand: observe active population → compute capacity → admit/withhold another S1 → observe completion → refill. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine from live task occupancy whether another subordinate S1 may enter the active set now. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party /flows controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Background.launch; per-task process/status records; flowsmaxparallel; countrunning; countqueued; launchquota; fillphaseslots; 500 ms status polling. - Closure path: current child-process statuses → running/queued counts → free-slot computation → launch or withhold queued child → changed active population → next poll observes the resulting occupancy. - Boundary reachability: /flows is a documented shipped product command and launches first-party swarm-code binaries; no application-authored coordinator is required beyond supplying workflow data. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user/model supplies prompts/workflow content, but the material attenuation decision is mechanically derived from current occupancy and the configured cap. Removing all model judgment does not remove the admission controller, so the function is code-owned. - Evidence: src/Flows.sw; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the credited S2 relation is resource/concurrency attenuation. No claim is made that /flows autonomously negotiates semantic conflicts, filesystem overlap or contradictory goals among concurrently running children.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate the current set and lifecycle of subordinate workflow commitments by maintaining the whole /flows phase/task state, admitting queued commitments into execution, advancing phases only after current commitments terminate and marking the organization complete or aborted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current workload population, queued versus running commitments, child completion/failure, phase dependencies/order, active-capacity changes and user-requested workflow abort. - Whole-system current view: Flows state contains the complete workflow phase list and every task&#39;s label, prompt/model, child id, current status, exit code, timing and execution statistics. refreshallphases polls the status of all launched tasks across all phases before current-control decisions are made. - Current-control decision scope: the controller launches the first/current phase, selects which queued commitments become active under the current cap, detects when all tasks in the current phase are terminal, starts the next phase, declares the whole flow done after the last phase, and changes the organizational state to aborted on the supported stop signal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which current workflow commitments execute now and when the organization may transition between current operational phases based on observed child state. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party /flows controller operating over an operator/user-authored workflow definition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: initstate; per-phase/task state; spawnphasetasks; fillphaseslots; refreshallphases; allagentsdone; phase transition logic; stop-file abort; Background process records/logs. - Closure path: all-current task/process state → deterministic controller refreshes and evaluates phase/capacity conditions → launch/withhold/advance/done/abort decision → child/phase state changes → next controller poll observes the changed whole-flow current state. - Boundary reachability: /flows is a standard documented execution surface implemented in the pinned repository and launches the product&#39;s own headless runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the workflow author supplies objectives/order, but current lifecycle decisions do not require a model to judge whether a phase is complete or which queued task fits a free slot; code owns those decisions from live state. The function is therefore C, not A. - Evidence: src/Flows.sw; src/background.sw; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a workflow-current-control S3, not an autonomous strategic manager. The workflow topology/prompts are supplied ahead of time, and current decisions are deterministic lifecycle control over that declared commitment structure.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material boundary-reachable complementary audit function with both sufficiently independent challenge and a returned corrective-control closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the experimental council can surface architecture, risk and operational disagreements, but no S3-specific corrective-return loop is closed into production control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a complementary auditor whose verdict changes current operational commitments. - Decision owner: not established at S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scripts/council.sh; three bounded read-only panel agents; no-tools judge; council-specific ToolRegistry execution contexts; tests/review scripts and ordinary coding/test tools. - Closure path: incomplete. Independent panels → judge synthesis → printed advisory output is established; judge verdict → authoritative corrective change in the primary Agent or /flows controller is not wired in the frozen product. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the council supplies real independent model judgments, but independence alone is insufficient. A human or some separate caller must interpret/use the final recommendation; swarm-code does not first-party route that result back into a corrective operational decision. - Evidence: scripts/council.sh; src/ToolRegistry.sw; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the council is stronger evidence than ordinary unit tests because its panelists are independent model actors with narrowed direct repository access; the negative state is specifically due to missing returned corrective closure, not lack of review independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective intelligence loop that develops and adopts adaptations to swarm-code&#39;s present organizational capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: scheduling, web access, memory/session search, skills, profiles, MCP and recurring prompts can broaden present execution or defer it in time, but they do not close future-oriented organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an S4 actor. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scheduler; persistent memory; session FTS; skills; web tools; MCP; provider profiles; cron prompts; trajectory export. - Closure path: no environment/future sensing → prospective option generation/evaluation → adopted change to current organizational capability/S3 loop was found. The scheduler merely compares time against operator-authored schedules and fire-and-forgets a predetermined prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task search/memory and scheduled execution reuse configured capabilities. They do not create a separate actor that observes emerging external/future conditions, develops an organizational adaptation and closes its adoption into current control. - Evidence: src/scheduler.sw; README.md; session/memory/skills modules in the pinned tree. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can schedule a prompt such as &quot;review open PRs&quot; and a model may reason prospectively inside that future S1 run; this is not a persistent first-party S4 closure over the product organization itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: trust decisions, permissions, execution contexts, hard command denies, remote-endpoint opt-in, project configuration restrictions and hook/tool policies bound operation but do not constitute runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5-level identity or ultimate policy. - Decision owner: developer/operator-authored configuration and trust choices remain outside a qualifying runtime S5 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolExecutor; ToolRegistry; trust/untrust configuration; project-config restriction; hardline command blocklist; context allow-lists; permissions/hooks; SWARMCODEALLOWREMOTE and other environment/config flags. - Closure path: absent at S5 level. Externally authored policy → deterministic enforcement is present; runtime identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative policy revision → returned organization-wide policy is not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models and deterministic controllers operate inside the policy envelope but do not own the legitimate right to redefine swarm-code&#39;s identity or ultimate constitutional rules. Operator trust/permission choices are external governance rather than product-owned S5. - Evidence: src/ToolExecutor.sw; src/ToolRegistry.sw; README.md; SECURITY.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strict fail-closed enforcement is important safety infrastructure but enforcement strength is not equivalent to S5 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a21178777039b6b5176ed68759b59c7f72bd526/assessments/swarm-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a21178777039b6b5176ed68759b59c7f72bd526\">9a21178 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T22:12:29Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CrewBeeLab/Waggle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Waggle</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-27\">2026-09-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CrewBeeLab/Waggle/tree/db9e3d3aed66714d665fd1c5c40d66534bf44ed7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision db9e3d3aed66714d665fd1c5c40d66534bf44ed7\">db9e3d3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform the substantive model-driven task of the governed AgentRun by choosing task-relevant actions/tools from current context, consuming tool observations and continuing until a terminal answer/state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user/product task input; model uncertainty; changing workspace/tool results; allowed capability set; model/tool failures; confirmation outcomes; bounded context and cancellation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific action/tool call and arguments from the current conversation/observations, then decide whether further actions are required or the run can complete. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor instantiated through Waggle&#39;s first-party production PiCoreAdapter operating mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external Pi Core loop primitive; Waggle Model Gateway; packed context/system prompt; Waggle tool wrappers; RuntimeToolExecutor; RunPermissionBoundary; Permission Guard; Workspace Guard; event sink; durable run lifecycle and cancellation. - Closure path: AgentRun input/context -&gt; Waggle PiCoreAdapter.execute() constructs model/context/tools -&gt; model chooses a tool/action -&gt; Waggle tool wrapper enters RuntimeToolExecutor -&gt; governed tool result returns as Pi/model observation -&gt; subsequent model turn changes action or produces terminal text -&gt; Waggle completes the run and persists evidence. - Boundary reachability: the README&#39;s documented run/HTTP path starts a real task through the Control Plane, and current-implementation.md identifies local PiCoreAdapter.execute() through Pi Core as current executable production-default runtime behavior rather than target architecture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Pi Core supplies generic loop mechanics and Waggle supplies policy/lifecycle plumbing, but neither deterministic layer preselects the contextual task action. Removing the model actor while retaining those mechanisms would not yield materially the same tool/argument/termination choices. The external loop library is an implementation dependency, not a separately deployed worker organization whose VSM functions are inherited. - Evidence: README.md; docs/development/current-implementation.md; packages/runtime-pi/src/pi-core-adapter.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: product-specific tools and final business effects remain outside Waggle. The S1 claim covers task decisions within the capabilities exposed to the governed run, not business authority that the README explicitly reserves to the product.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no qualifying inter-S1 coordination function is established in the current generic standard distribution at the assessed AgentRun recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the repository has Project/Session/Run state, queues/workers and future parent-child concepts, but no current first-party witness establishes distinct S1 operational units plus a concrete interaction disturbance and S2-specific attenuation relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/run state; queue/worker records; run orchestration; Tool Proxy; shared evidence; planned parent/child topology. - Closure path: no current distinct-S1 disturbance -&gt; S2-specific coordination judgment -&gt; changed behaviour of the affected S1 units is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary queueing, shared records and run orchestration coordinate software execution, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not award S2/C from generic communication/scheduling primitives. The architecture explicitly places parent-child orchestration in target evolution rather than current executable truth. - Evidence: docs/development/architecture.md; docs/development/current-implementation.md; packages/core/src/runtime/run-orchestrator.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a later implemented parent/child/multi-run organization or downstream product can create a different recursion that requires separate assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate first-party whole-system current-control decision loop is established beyond the governed S1 run and deterministic enforcement of preselected constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool risk, actor/package/workspace mismatch, expired authority, model/tool availability, cancellation, lease/watchdog failures and confirmation-required effects are regulated mechanically or by a local parent decision, but the frozen boundary does not show a manager selecting current priorities/resources/commitments/constraints for the whole from a current operational view. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for a qualifying S3 function. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable RunPermissionBoundary; Permission Guard; RuntimeToolExecutor; run statuses; worker scheduling/leases; watchdog/recovery; cancellation; user/product confirmations; package/run configuration. - Closure path: configured authority -&gt; deterministic allow/ask/deny and lifecycle enforcement -&gt; current run behavior changes. This is strong enforcement, but no distinct whole-system S3 discretion/feedback loop is supplied. - Boundary reachability: all mechanisms are present in current execution, but they remain pre-authored enforcement/lifecycle paths rather than a separately owned current-control function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model proposes an S1 action; the guard applies already-selected rules; a human/product can approve one specific effect. Removing the putative manager leaves the same deterministic rule enforcement because there is no autonomous manager to remove. Ordinary one-action approval does not satisfy the Methodology&#39;s parent-governed S3 witness. - Whole-system current view: Waggle exposes run/session/events/evidence/attention state, but visibility is not itself a current-control decision right. - Current-control decision scope: cancellation, confirmation and permission effects are local/run-lifecycle decisions; no qualifying resource/priority/accountability/synergy intervention for the whole is established. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/permissions/run-permission-boundary.ts; packages/core/src/permissions/permission-guard.ts; apps/cli/src/run-confirmation-flow.ts; docs/development/current-implementation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: product-owned business authority is real, but the evidence at this recursion concerns individual business-effect confirmation/final write or preconfigured rules, not an S3 whole-system current-control function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary audit judgment with corrective return into subsequent operation is established in the current runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Waggle records trace, audit, evidence, permission decisions, artifacts and package eval/release state, but these surfaces are produced by or validate the same governed execution/lifecycle paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an independent auditor that challenges operational claims from complementary access and returns a correction. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace/audit/evidence stores; governed-run evidence; validation/eval types; package registry validation; confirmation records; hash/provenance checks. - Closure path: operation/control -&gt; records/checks/evidence; no independent audit actor/judgment -&gt; corrective instruction -&gt; changed and rechecked operation loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: logging, deterministic contract validation, package lifecycle checks and human confirmation are not independent operational audit by themselves. - Evidence: docs/development/current-implementation.md; packages/core/src/evals/index.ts; packages/core/src/registry/agent-package-registry-service.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream AgentPackages may define reviewers/evals, but application-specific reviewers are not credited to generic Waggle without a first-party standard path that closes independence and corrective feedback.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environment-facing adaptation loop is established in the current standard operating mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider catalogs, package versions, eval declarations, memory and product integrations can change across deployments/runs, but changes are operator/developer-authored or deterministic lifecycle state rather than autonomous sensing of external/future distinctions and option development. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for selecting and adopting a future capability/posture based on environmental intelligence. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPackage versions; validate/eval/release/rollback/deprecate APIs; model catalog/connections; persistent context/memory; proposals; development hot reload. - Closure path: operator/developer supplies new package/config/version -&gt; Waggle validates/releases/uses it. No environment sensing -&gt; prospective options -&gt; autonomous adaptation judgment -&gt; returned changed present capability loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: package lifecycle machinery can promote or roll back supplied artifacts, but the frozen runMinimumEval() does not itself generate capability alternatives or adapt from external distinctions. Persistence/reuse and model selection are not S4 by name. - Evidence: packages/core/src/registry/agent-package-registry-service.ts; docs/development/architecture.md; docs/development/current-implementation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: AgentPackage lifecycle provides useful construction/release infrastructure, but Methodology C still requires the S4 function itself to be established; a generic package/version API is not enough.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop closes at the Waggle-managed run recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Product/AgentPackage policy, allowed/denied tools, user/workspace permissions, model/provider restrictions, confirmation rules and business ownership constrain the run, but ultimate purpose/business identity remains externally authored. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define/revise the ultimate purpose, identity or top-level normative policy of the operating organization itself. - Decision owner: external product/operator/developer; no qualifying first-party S5 closure is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPackage manifest/profile/policy; Project/ProductIntegration records; RunPermissionBoundary; confirmation rules; user identity; product-owned final writes; package release state. - Closure path: product/operator supplies purpose/policy -&gt; Waggle compiles/enforces it. No identity-level dispute/proposal -&gt; authoritative S5 judgment -&gt; returned ultimate policy -&gt; subsequent operation loop closes inside Waggle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the README explicitly reserves business rules, user identity, confirmation UX and final writes to the product. This is parent/external authority, but the evidenced runtime return paths are configuration and individual effect authorization, not a genuine identity-level deliberation/decision path required for S5=P. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/permissions/run-permission-boundary.ts; apps/cli/src/run-confirmation-flow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream product organization may have legitimate S5 authority and use Waggle as enforcement infrastructure. That parent organization is a different system-in-focus; its authority is not converted into generic Waggle S5 merely because Waggle can enforce supplied policy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/814b7d3c5c0fa49a2d02d4bd47b447e41128c322/assessments/waggle.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 814b7d3c5c0fa49a2d02d4bd47b447e41128c322\">814b7d3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T00:51:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/madarco/agentbox\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentBox</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/madarco/agentbox/tree/463a6a0a20176147f0b0dbfa8a261aba5e43cbd6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 463a6a0a20176147f0b0dbfa8a261aba5e43cbd6\">463a6a0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Supported coding agents autonomously perform coding work inside AgentBox-managed boxes. As with other coding-agent control planes in the Index, external model/backend cognition does not turn the assembled harness operation into C when the standard distribution runs autonomous S1 executions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. The design document explicitly states that preventing boxes from fighting over the same files is manual at the pin. Shared task records, assignment fields, isolated boxes and reconciliation are useful infrastructure, but no implemented S2-specific path is shown that detects/attenuates a concrete inter-S1 collision or dependency disturbance and feeds the result back into subsequent box behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. Manager sessions, authoritative task state and lifecycle APIs expose current information/control mechanisms, but the manager&#39;s own brief is explicitly planned and absent. The pinned system therefore does not establish a first-party actor with whole-system current view plus discretionary authority over priorities/resources/commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Timeline and PR observation surfaces do not establish a sufficiently independent first-party audit judgment with corrective closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. No external-and-prospective organizational intelligence/adaptation loop is implemented at the reviewed boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. User objectives, task creation, approval surfaces and external authority are not themselves a runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure loop. The earlier proposal over-promoted outer human authority to P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f17df7784f951d191718bb7504b7770132a18a8a/assessments/agentbox.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f17df7784f951d191718bb7504b7770132a18a8a\">f17df77 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T17:38:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/formiat/multi-agent-orchestrator-rs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">multi-agent-orchestrator-rs</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/formiat/multi-agent-orchestrator-rs/tree/ea6c114a40c005256d125984b3d77cc758bdc8fe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ea6c114a40c005256d125984b3d77cc758bdc8fe\">ea6c114 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Per-request planning, investigation or implementation of software tasks, producing investigation/plan documents or local code changes with feedback-directed repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable repository behavior, complex task requirements, defects, missing artifacts, regressions, rejected reviewer checks and uncertain provider session continuity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured external-provider executor agent reasons about the specific task, inspects the workspace and edits/commits or writes the required artifact; a separately bound reviewer agent independently examines actual changes, chooses an evidence-backed YAML accept/revise/blocked/disagreement decision and sends concrete improvement feedback. The first-party Rust gate only interprets structured decisions and deterministic conditions, and retries the executor on revise. - Decision owner: The task-dependent choices and corrections are owned by the two configured AI agent sessions (Claude/OpenCode/Codex) under first-party prompt templates and provider dispatch in this distributed execution mode. The Rust orchestrator is the deterministic harness controller; the human supplies the prompt, target repo, model/provider options, session identities and network policies. These external provider tools are indispensable executors, not a first-party in-process model implementation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source-owned workflow templates, CLI input and session lock/binding, transport files and fingerprints, provider subprocess adapters, FSM monitor, reviewer YAML schema and quality gate, Git checks, retry cap, batch task-file queue. - Closure path: CLI workspace/prompt and preexisting sessions → first-party context preparation + executor prompt → provider-session executor makes and reports task changes → first-party monitor/transport → independent reviewer-session checks code/files/tests → parsed review decision → Rust quality gate closes success or dispatches feedback into a later executor round. Batch mode repeats per queued task, not autonomously planning a new organization. - Boundary reachability: Main binary invokes first-party orchestrator; current orchestrator phases dispatch the two supported provider sessions with owned templates and transport; accept/revise flow in quality gate terminates or re-enters executor dispatch. Batch binary invokes the same orchestration workflow for each task file. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the configured agent sessions eliminates the discretionary investigation/coding and semantic review; Rust cannot invent equivalent task-specific plans, code or critique. Conversely removing the Rust FSM leaves uncoordinated provider sessions without shipped restart/review-contract/gate behavior. Agent discretion is first-party integrated at the supported runtime boundary, not Rust inference. - Evidence: README.md; src/main.rs; src/orchestrator/mod.rs; src/workflow/executor.rs; src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/dispatch.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/output.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/lib.rs; src/providers/mod.rs; src/yamlcheck.rs - Basis: explicit and structural — executable prompt/dispatch/review/feedback chain - Confidence: High for source-wired agentic S1; provider session runtime effects not live-tested. - Caveats: Requires two separate existing authenticated provider sessions, CLI binaries and an appropriate clean Git worktree. No provider inference or real target modifications were performed; the repo&#39;s documented behavior and first-party integration code, not measured output quality, ground this result.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No sustained coordination function shown that attenuates actual inter-S1 oscillation or mutual disturbance among multiple independently viable operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An executor may miss a requirement and reviewer may request revisions; batches contain several sequential software tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer feedback governs acceptance/revision for the same coding task, not mutual operating interference between separate viable S1 departments. Sequential batch runner selects the next numbered task after completion but does not maintain interdependent S1 mutual constraints. - Decision owner: Reviewer session owns a task-local judgment; Rust quality gate routes retry and the queue determines sequence. No distinct S2 variety-attenuating owner between viable units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role separation and transport protocol, review YAML accept/revise contract, fixed FSM, sequential batch task processing, per-session locks. - Closure path: Executor work → reviewer feedback → same executor&#39;s corrective next round → terminal result; not S1-A disturbance → S2 negotiation/coordination → modified concurrent S1-B and S1-A behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Executor and reviewer are asymmetric production and quality-control roles in one work cell. Review is not coordination of two ongoing independently viable systems. Multiple queue entries are tasks rather than independently operating environmental departments. - Evidence: src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/workflow/executor.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/lib.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/queue.rs; src/orchestrator/mod.rs - Basis: bounded negative with positive task-coordination hypothesis checked - Confidence: High at bounded coding-workflow recursion. - Caveats: Teams running several instances could externally coordinate across products, but no shipped cross-instance organizational S2/feedback loop is assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately justified whole-system present management of continuing viable operational units and binding cross-unit resources/commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider hangs, malformed YAML, dirty worktree, regressions, policy violations and failed task deliveries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Rust decides state transitions/retries, scoring thresholds, termination, no-progress caps, and error reporting from a fixed configuration. Reviewer decides if one task is acceptable. Human selects CLI constraints before running; no actor evaluates and directs whole-current multi-unit production/resource/accountability trade-offs. - Decision owner: Task reviewer agent for bounded product gate, deterministic Rust FSM for mechanical retry/failure and user for input policies. None is shown exercising the organizational S3 management right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: FSM run states, monitoring/probe diagnostics, reviewer schema/quality score, session locks, workspace clean preflight, provider options and network policy. - Closure path: One task/reviewer output → deterministic gate/retry/failure → same task executor or terminal JSON; no plural operational-unit management returns with allocations or policy reconciliations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A quality gate over one deliverable is not whole-current S3 merely because it blocks acceptance. Network policies and retry thresholds originate outside the agent, and code enforces rather than autonomously decides organization-wide management. - Evidence: src/orchestrator/mod.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; src/fsm.rs; src/state.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/preflight.rs; src/cli.rs; src/orchestrator/monitor.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/lib.rs - Basis: bounded negative after model, deterministic and human paths - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An operator could use the tool inside a multi-team organization, but that parent&#39;s present resource-management authority is not wired as an assessed first-party mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No distinct complementary investigation of an S3 management view or its executive claims against unfiltered operational reality with corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Executor self-report may misstate implementation quality, omit changed files, hide regressions or violate policies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The separately bound reviewer is explicitly required to inspect workspace artifacts, code, diffs and risks independently and issue revise/accept/block decisions. This is strong task-level quality verification, but it samples executor output, not claims in a distinct organizational S3 present-management account. - Decision owner: Independent reviewer agent owns a real S1 quality-review decision. The deterministic gate applies it to the same work item; no S3 mandate/recipient at the current whole-system management recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer prompts with no-trust requirement, workspace/commit checks, YAML structured findings, review repair and procedural error gates. - Closure path: Executor artifact and claims → independent reviewer evidence → YAML judgment → executor correction / terminal; not S3 management self-report → independently sampled unit reality → corrective report into continuing S3 controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Do not discard a real independent reviewer: its audit-like behavior is positive evidence for robust S1 control. Its measured object, decision scope, and corrective recipient remain task delivery, not S3 managerial communications or an independent S3 audit function. - Evidence: src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/workflow/executor.rs; src/yamlcheck.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/output.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; src/orchestrator/mod.rs - Basis: explicit source evidence of task verification, negative S3 function adjudication - Confidence: High with fixed recursion and current management assessment. - Caveats: One could change the boundary or introduce a manager whose reports are audited; no such parent pathway exists as shipped code.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No continuing future-environment intelligence with accepted strategic change to the organization’s capability or policies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown technical constraints may emerge in investigations; provider models may vary, external Git/Notion/remote systems may change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An executor can research and create an investigation or implementation plan for the current user-supplied task. Model override, provider selection, network policy and prompt/workflow type are operator inputs; neither S4 agent nor parent accepts a strategy governing future work of the whole system. - Decision owner: Configured executor model for immediate task analysis and user/developer for CLI/provider policy; no standing outward/future strategy owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Investigate and plan workflow types, provider CLI adapters, project profiles, Notion/remote access protocols, task wrappers and current workspace discovery. - Closure path: Current request → case investigation/PLAN.md → reviewer quality feedback → file artifact/implementation. No outside trends → future strategic options → legitimate acceptance → changed future ongoing organizational operating capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The words &#39;plan&#39;, &#39;investigate&#39;, and &#39;research&#39; can denote future thinking about a software change but refer to scoped S1 user work, not the viable organization&#39;s environmental strategy and accepted renewal. - Evidence: src/workflow/executor.rs; src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/cli.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/lib.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/preflight.rs; README.md - Basis: bounded negative with task research positive hypothesis evaluated - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Outputs may inform human strategic decisions externally; no first-party S4 acceptance/return is provided.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate organizational identity/purpose-policy determination or legitimate parent adjudication/return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting user requirements, provider selection, remote network safety, quality thresholds, blocked/irreconcilable reviewer outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: External operator sets workflow, providers, sessions and remote network policy. Static Rust/embedded prompts enforce no-forbidden remote actions, reviewer acceptance rules, hard-block stopping and manual next-action reporting; no actor decides the organization&#39;s enduring constitutional purposes and returns revised identity policy into execution. - Decision owner: External human/developer for configuration/policy input; deterministic runtime enforces it and reviewer rejects violations. No first-party S5 agent or legitimate parent identity-policy role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI flags, remote-network policy enum, baked-in reviewer instructions and quality thresholds, session locks, safety gates and terminal error reporting. - Closure path: Operator-provided constraints → task-level enforcement/stop or reviewer verdict → terminal report for operator; no ultimate identity issue → valid top authority resolves enduring purpose → adopted constitutional policy changes subsequent system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A strong deterministic permission check, a quality standard, a disagreement outcome and an external operator do not by themselves form S5; they lack the ultimate-policy object/authority/closure. - Evidence: src/cli.rs; src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/workflow/executor.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; src/orchestrator/mod.rs; README.md - Basis: bounded negative after parent-mode investigation - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The user can govern their own external organization, but it is not a first-party parent-governed constitutional mode of this released orchestrator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7479e63272154f37cb01a8fb89874edce89067b1/assessments/mu/multi-agent-orchestrator-rs.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7479e63272154f37cb01a8fb89874edce89067b1\">7479e63 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T01:39:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cubeplexai/cubeloop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CubeLoop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cubeplexai/cubeloop/tree/eb798f9a20c1f516d6a59a991a63a9d4c630f4bf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision eb798f9a20c1f516d6a59a991a63a9d4c630f4bf\">eb798f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Goal-facing model-selected task operations through first-party bound model/tool feedback, with optional ephemeral delegated subagents and current-goal evaluator continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New user requests, changing tool execution results, exceptions, model fallback, context window pressure, streaming interruptions, human tool admission, pending task state and checkpoints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured bound model chooses which registered tool call to issue or when to finish; child-bound-model runs choose task-local actions; the optional goal evaluator returns an achieved/unmet verdict and injects current-task feedback. - Decision owner: Model in a first-party Agent loop has per-goal tool-choice discretion; host configures tools, middleware, provider, model and approval. The ephemeral child model is an S1 task extension, not a separate metasystem. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runagentloop / runloopinner model streaming, tool-cycle and tool result append; Agent.prompt session, middleware callbacks, concurrently executable tool calls, checkpoint/resume and provider fallback; GoalMiddleware onrunend verification and child delegation. - Closure path: User message → BoundModel stream/generate → assistant tool choices → first-party tool execution/exception reporting → observed result becomes subsequent context → model next choice or final answer; child delegation returns an ephemeral child output. Optional goal evaluator can feed a not-yet-achieved reason back and restart current-goal work. - Boundary reachability: Published Agent(model=BoundModel, tools=[...], middleware=[...]) and prompt() call the runtime; actual Anthropic/OpenAI adapters exist. Child model execution, GoalMiddleware and provider failover are optional but wired first-party. This is not inferred from docs or test fixtures. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without configured model discretion the remaining session/checkpoint/approval machinery could not select novel goal-sensitive tool paths. A real model must be configured, but the first-party harness provides reachable running infrastructure. - Evidence: cubeloop/agent/agent.py; cubeloop/agent/loop.py; cubeloop/agent/tools.py; cubeloop/providers/base.py; cubeloop/providers/openai.py; cubeloop/providers/anthropic.py; cubeloop/middleware/subagents.py; cubeloop/middleware/goal.py; cubeloop/session/session.py. - Basis: Explicit structural runtime wiring, corroborated by runnable examples; no task-quality experiment run. - Confidence: High for S1 model/feedback architecture. - Caveats: Model provider availability, model correctness and external app tool semantics are not reproduced. Ephemeral subagent capability does not automatically establish a stable second viable organizational S1 department.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independently governed inter-operating-unit coordination that dampens demonstrated conflicts among distinct viable S1 departments at this framework recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent tools/child requests, inconsistent results and user steering can disrupt one task, but distributed multi-department interference is not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent tool concurrency and deferred dispatch decide task execution order; child subagents return findings to parent. No S2-specific decision regarding shared commitments among separately viable departmental S1s. - Decision owner: Caller-configured parent model and runtime sequencing/dispatch; no separately identified S2 coordinating owner across viable S1s. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Parallel/sequential tool execution, SubagentMiddleware, shared tools, deferred tool groups, todo guard and execution-session queue. - Closure path: Tool and child outcomes return to the same parent task; session consumer ordering and tool batching do not close an inter-S1 constraint/oscillation feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Subagent instances are ephemeral task-service workers with model and tools, not independently continuing viable organizational departments under a common reciprocal operating regime. Task-level scheduling is not S2. - Evidence: cubeloop/middleware/subagents.py; cubeloop/agent/loop.py; cubeloop/agent/tools.py; cubeloop/deferred/middleware.py; cubeloop/middleware/todo.py; cubeloop/session/session.py. - Basis: Bounded negative at the shipped SDK Agent/task boundary. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external multi-agent product might use CubeLoop as its substrate and supply real inter-unit coordination; that external arrangement is not within this source-bound assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party current whole-system operational-management loop owning a portfolio of independently viable S1s, resource choices and returned departmental obligations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Run failures, tool-call admission, concurrent execution, user controls, agent steering/follow-ups and task checkpoint errors threaten present task completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent chooses goal-local actions, caller/HITL middleware may approve or deny one tool, user can steer/abort/resume. ExecutionSession arbitrates one agent&#39;s attempts, not discretionary current-resource allocation across S1 departments. - Decision owner: External host/user selects tool exposure, policy, execution mode and the model; middleware supplies admission guard; no distinct whole-current S3 organizational owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ApprovalPolicyMiddleware, ConfirmToolCallMiddleware, session single-attempt lock, persisted input receipts, middleware todo finalization guards, checkpoint/recovery. - Closure path: Operation request → approval/denial or interruption → same task accepts/block/resume; no aggregate viable-S1 status → S3 authoritative allocation → changed operating units and feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Interactive HITL decisions and deterministic session arbitration are concrete but functionally local. A configurable human approval channel is not by itself an S3=P parent-managed organization. - Evidence: cubeloop/hitl/middleware.py; cubeloop/hitl/channel.py; cubeloop/session/session.py; cubeloop/agent/agent.py; cubeloop/middleware/todo.py; cubeloop/checkpointer/base.py. - Basis: Bounded absence after checking supported HITL, session and delegated child modes. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An embedding application can place an external manager above multiple CubeLoop agents; its decisions cannot be counted as this SDK&#39;s native S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary independently owned direct-operating-reality audit with correction returned to a distinct S3 owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current runs can produce misleading completion statements or obscure tool faults and paused approvals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: GoalMiddleware optionally asks a separate evaluator model whether a single task goal is met from a recent transcript; it may resume that very task. Tracing records spans and HITL history; neither supplies independent system-operating reality discovery to current S3 management. - Decision owner: Optional evaluator BoundModel is owned by the goal middleware and governs only completion of a given requested goal, not an S3 audit mandate. Tracing is host-attached instrumentation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalMiddleware verdict, tracing/Recorder/Tracer, tool event streams, todo finalization guard and session receipts. - Closure path: Transcript → goal judge → goal-current-task continuation/stop. Trace → observability sink. There is no independently checked operational reality → adjudicated management correction → S3 decision returned to operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even though a second model evaluates task outcome, it sees the supplied conversation, not direct organizational reality under independent audit authority. Mere judge separation does not establish S3; there is no first-party S3 manager receiving an audit challenge. - Evidence: cubeloop/middleware/goal.py; cubeloop/tracing/tracer.py; cubeloop/middleware/todo.py; cubeloop/session/session.py; cubeloop/hitl/middleware.py. - Basis: Bounded negative with explicit inspection of the plausible evaluator/audit path. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: If deployed with externally mandated trace reviewers and management correction, a bigger composite boundary may have S3; it is not shipped by the bounded SDK.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped owner and closure for future-facing external intelligence and accepted reconfiguration of subsequent organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider failures, task complexity, rising context costs, new task types or changing operational surroundings would motivate later platform adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: BoundModel fallback picks an alternate configured provider on failure; child agents handle current delegated work; goal evaluator continues current task, todo updates current task steps. None adopts a new future strategy/method for subsequent operating runs. - Decision owner: Host developer supplies fallback chain, middleware and tools in advance; no first-party prospective adaptive strategy process makes and returns a decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: FallbackBoundModel, goal evaluation feedback, task todo plan, optional compaction, cached state and middleware hook plumbing. - Closure path: Failure→configured fallback inside same request, goal judge→same request continuation; no boundary-spanning outside surveillance → future option → legitimate acceptance → changed next-run operating rules. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Runtime retrying/fallback or agent revising a TODO list within one goal is S1 adaptation, not VSM outside-and-then S4. The dev/spec/plan files document future engineering intent, not shipped executable S4 ownership. - Evidence: cubeloop/providers/fallback.py; cubeloop/middleware/goal.py; cubeloop/middleware/todo.py; cubeloop/agent/agent.py; cubeloop/middleware/base.py; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting shipped candidate future-strategy routes. - Confidence: High for current packaged modes. - Caveats: Cross-run learning introduced by downstream host code is not evidenced by the pinned first-party framework; a future release may change this.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate identity/ultimate-purpose policy-authoring owner with authoritative return into the framework&#39;s subsequent organizational operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operators must decide which tools and models to expose, when to require human approval and how to handle agents&#39; data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Tool approvals can approve/deny/edit one call; application integrator configures system prompt, permissions and middleware. No user/parent procedure for binding constitutional policy or identity changes across viable S1s is implemented. - Decision owner: User/integrator owns purpose and application governance outside this SDK; runtime&#39;s policy callbacks only execute configured local task admission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent(systemprompt=...,model=...,tools=...), HITL ApprovalDecision (Approve/Deny/AskUser), checkpointer, tool registry and examples. - Closure path: User/developer config → bounded agent operation; no ultimate mission/policy question → legitimate S5 decision → identity/charter enforcement returned to subsequent organizational actors. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A human editing a tool request, setting task goals or changing a config knob is not a first-party S5 parent-governed mode. There is no preserved global decision mandate, identity-level dispute route or returned policy for an autonomous organization. - Evidence: cubeloop/agent/agent.py; cubeloop/hitl/middleware.py; cubeloop/hitl/channel.py; cubeloop/session/session.py; README.md. - Basis: Bounded absence across published host-configuration and HITL modes. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Organizational governance of CubePlex product customers can provide S5 outside this Index review boundary; cannot borrow it into CubeLoop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/661548addf330e3e588b06e5d8f19b68d1dfff42/assessments/cu/cubeloop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 661548addf330e3e588b06e5d8f19b68d1dfff42\">661548a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T23:42:27Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sausheong/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sausheong/harness/tree/1e5b6599bbc68519e83d156150d70f793653ca7d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1e5b6599bbc68519e83d156150d70f793653ca7d\">1e5b659 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended agent work through a model-driven decision/action/observation loop with first-party tool execution and continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/host goals, evolving session state, tool/service/file observations, model uncertainty, tool errors, provider responses, context pressure and runtime budget constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive answer or tool action in light of current context and returned tool observations. - Decision owner: the autonomous model-driven agent actor running inside Harness&#39;s first-party Runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime.Run / RunTurn, provider abstraction, tool registry/executor, permission checker, session persistence, compaction, budgets, result spilling, fallback model and lifecycle hooks. - Closure path: host/user goal → model turn → model-selected tool call or answer → Harness validates/executes the allowed tool → tool result is appended to session → later model turn sees the observation and revises/continues → terminal answer or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: Runtime.Run and RunTurn are the core shipped library API and require no repository-development actor; a host only supplies the external provider/tools/session expected by the library contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code transports, bounds and executes actions, but it does not choose the substantive next task action. Removing the model actor leaves execution machinery without the open-ended operational decision loop. - Evidence: runtime/runturn.go; runtime/runtime.go; runtime/builder.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the model/provider itself is environment. S1 credit rests on Harness owning the first-party action/execution/observation continuation path around that model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party coordination function was established that attenuates a specific interference/oscillation between distinct S1 units at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Harness can create parent and child Runtime units, but the reviewed standard distribution does not identify a concrete inter-unit conflict and a function-specific coordination judgment/feedback path for resolving it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task delegation, separate child sessions, recursion depth caps, tool-call concurrency classification, per-Runtime runMu, persistent-session writer leases and shared event forwarding. - Closure path: delegation returns child output to the parent, and locks/serialization protect runtime data structures, but no qualifying inter-S1 disturbance → coordination response → changed later S1 behavior loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: delegation and transport are not S2 by themselves. The strongest collision-like mechanisms are deterministic safety primitives: the task tool is marked non-concurrency-safe because parallel child-event forwarding is untested, while persistent writer leases protect one session from concurrent writers rather than coordinating the distinct parent/child S1 units that normally use separate sessions. - Evidence: tool/task.go; runtime/subagent.go; session/lease.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a caller application could construct multi-agent collision regulation around these primitives; generic programmability is not enough for C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-system current-control organ was established at the declared application recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Harness bounds individual Runtime work through turn/token/time/tool concurrency, permissions, steering and cancellation, but these do not form a whole-system view and decision loop over multiple current commitments/resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose or revise current organization-wide allocations, commitments, priorities, constraints or interventions from a whole-system view. - Decision owner: not established. Host/application code chooses configuration and may supply steering; deterministic Harness code enforces those choices at the Runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: budgets, max turns/depth/tool concurrency, permissions, session locking, runtime cancellation, lifecycle hooks and optional steering source. - Closure path: configured limits and user corrections can change or stop one Runtime trajectory, but no standard whole-system current view → S3 judgment → organization-wide current-operation change path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hard enforcement does not imply ownership. The WithSteering contract explicitly leaves queue ownership, pending/delivered state and reconciliation to the host and inserts corrections into one run at execution boundaries; it is not a fleet/whole-system management organ. - Evidence: runtime/STEERING.md; runtime/runtime.go; runtime/partition.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host can build a manager above the library, but that external composition is outside the reviewed standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary-audit path over an operational claim was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary model/tool results may be wrong or incomplete, but the standard runtime does not independently inspect operational reality and return an audit judgment to current control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: traces, session history, hooks, errors, tool results, compaction state and the separate runtime.Review reviewer Runtime. - Closure path: ordinary tool observations return to S1 and Review can write future memory/skills, but neither forms a claim → materially complementary evidence → independent audit verdict → corrective current-control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime.Review is a distinct model pass, but its supplied prompts ask what should be remembered for future sessions rather than checking a producer&#39;s operational claim against independent evidence. It is credited under S4 instead of being relabeled as audit. - Evidence: runtime/review.go; runtime/reviewprompts.go; runtime/runtime.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a caller can provide a domain-specific ReviewSpec.Prompt and tools, but generic custom prompting is not a first-party S3-specific constructor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: convert evidence from completed user/environment interactions into durable future-facing memory or reusable skills that can alter later agent capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user preferences, project constraints and useful workflows discovered during operation can change or accumulate, making a fixed future agent context/capability progressively less fitted to later sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which completed-interaction distinctions are durable/reusable enough to save and choose whether to create/update future-facing memory or procedural skill content. - Decision owner: constructor path. When invoked, the dedicated reviewer model owns the content-selection/adaptation judgment; however the host application must explicitly install the OnStop trigger, shared stores and review toolset, so Harness does not supply a resident autonomous S4 organ by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime.Review, snapshot reviewer Runtime, ReviewPromptDefault / ReviewPromptVerbose, memory and skill tools/stores, origin tagging, RuntimeDeps.Skills, BuildRuntime skill-index construction and refreshSkills before later model requests. - Closure path: completed user/agent interaction → separately invoked reviewer reads the finished conversation → reviewer identifies a durable preference/project fact/reusable workflow → shared memory/skill write → later Runtime request refreshes the self-authored skill index / exposes load tools → subsequent operational model context and available procedural knowledge can change. - Boundary reachability: runtime.Review and the standard future-session prompts are shipped Runtime APIs with documented LifecycleHooks.OnStop wiring; the same repository ships memory/skill providers and an end-to-end example. The function-specific constructor is therefore product-reachable even though applications must opt in and compose it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: once composed, the reviewer model makes the substantive prospective selection rather than a fixed parser. The missing piece is standard autonomous installation/trigger ownership, so Methodology 0.3.6 publishes constructor C, not A. - Evidence: runtime/review.go; runtime/reviewprompts.go; runtime/builder.go; runtime/skillsrefresh.go; examples/self-improving-agent/main.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - External distinction: the standard reviewer prompt explicitly extracts changing user preferences and project facts from a completed interaction, treating user/project evidence outside the Runtime&#39;s fixed prior capability as distinctions worth carrying forward. - Future / prospective distinction: the prompt filters for information “worth remembering for future sessions” and reusable workflows/techniques rather than merely summarizing the completed task. - Adaptation option generated: reviewer-selected durable memory entries and named procedural skills representing future behavior/context/capability changes. - Path back into current capability / S3: reviewer writes into the parent&#39;s shared stores; BuildRuntime exposes memory/skills and embeds skill indices, while Runtime.Run refreshes self-authored skills before subsequent model requests, so later S1 operation can use the adaptation. - Caveats: memory alone would not establish S4. Credit rests on the explicit future-relevance judgment plus reusable-skill generation and a first-party return path into later model context. The opt-in caller wiring prevents A.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the declared application recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent specs, system prompts, permissions, budgets and memory files constrain operation, but the reviewed distribution does not define a genuine identity/constitutional issue and an authoritative decision loop that returns to govern the organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: caller/operator configuration supplies prompts, permissions and policy-like constraints; no qualifying S5 authority path is packaged by Harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSpec.SystemPrompt, MemoryFiles, permission checker, budgets, steering and caller-selected runtime configuration. - Closure path: configuration can alter later operation, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority judgment → returned governing-policy closure is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and permission gates are constraints, not S5 by themselves. runtime.Review writes memory/skills and does not receive an ultimate-policy mandate or authority to redefine the application&#39;s identity. - Evidence: runtime/builder.go; runtime/review.go; runtime/STEERING.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host application may establish its own parent governance around Harness; that would require assessment at the host application&#39;s recursion rather than attribution to this library.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/11c63c1307b12e1fb1dbc6c081f7fed561bf9b1c/assessments/harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 11c63c1307b12e1fb1dbc6c081f7fed561bf9b1c\">11c63c1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
